"Daren?" Hadrik tilts his head as we navigate the damp stone corridor, our boots splashing through shallow runoff. "You doing all right there, buddy?"

"I'm fine," Daren says without breaking stride, the plates of his heavy armor clanking in the enclosed space. "Why?"

To demonstrate his point, he gives his broadsword a casual underhand toss four paces ahead. The crossguard clatters against the wet flagstones, steel skittering with a hollow ring. Daren strides forward with an open gauntlet thrust toward the blade, reaches it, stoops to scoop the leather-wrapped hilt off the floor, and heaves it down the passage once more.

"Only that you've hurled that sword six times in three minutes, and I'm beginning to wonder about your head," Hadrik says, hitching the strap of his pack higher on his broad shoulder. "You can ask one of us to carry the steel if your arms are giving out from yesterday."

"My arms are fine," Daren says, scooping the hilt from the slick stones again. "I'm working on aura manipulation to pull the weapon back to my grip. The underground king mentioned the trick before we slept. Why did you think I was thrusting my palm out every time I threw it?"

A sharp rasp of laughter echoes from the golden amulet hanging against Flower's tunic. "Is that what that flailing was?" Arraka's voice bubbles up through the brass casing. "I never would have guessed in ten thousand years. The depths of your aura incompetence are truly breathtaking."

"Were you planning on offering anything useful, or should I just tune out the cackling and keep practicing?" Daren asks, wiping grit from his pommel.

"Keep practicing like that and the sun will burn out before you shift a pebble," Arraka says. "Spreading your aura wide is useless if you don't know how to condense it. That self-styled king already told you this trick relies on familiarity with the object. Stop forcing it with your palm like a beggar reaching for coin. Picture the steel in your mind and let loose five distinct currents from your fingertips until you feel them latch onto the pommel. It takes almost no effort."

"Five currents from my fingertips?" Daren frowns down at his calloused fingers. "What does that even mean? Are you making up words as you go?"

"I don't have the patience to nurse a grown man through basic concepts," Arraka snaps. "Hey, goblin! You understood me. Translate it into simple words for the blockhead."

"Right," Kelrim says, gathering the hem of his oversized white robes so they don't drag in the puddle. "I believe she means that prickling sensation in your fingers whenever you draw your aura inward. Remember that exact prickle, imagine it streaming like thin water jets from your fingertips, and cast them forward toward the blade on the ground. If you can't recall the feeling, suppress your aura for a moment to find the sensation again."

Daren stops in the center of the passage. The rest of us halt beside him, our breath misting in the subterranean chill. "All right," he mutters, rolling his shoulders. "Let's see."

He flings the broadsword down the tunnel once more. This time his hands stay low at his hips. He narrows his dark eyes at the fallen blade, his brow furrowing and his jaw set in fierce concentration.

Without warning, the steel snaps off the stone floor with a sharp crack. It zips through the air, hilt-first, and smacks with a loud, leathery slap straight into Daren's open right palm. He doesn't even have to adjust his stance.

Daren stares down at the weapon resting in his grip, his scar twitching in disbelief. "That's it? That's all it takes? What in the world? I thought this required weeks of drilling! If it's this straightforward, why isn't every warrior doing it?"

"Because it's not straightforward, you oaf," Arraka sneers. "You happen to have an instinctive knack for aura manipulation, otherwise you'd still be standing there looking foolish. Put a typical mage in your boots and they'd fail for a month. If you weren't such a stubborn rock when it comes to actual theory, you might even turn out competent."

choice(""Does this mean that Daren could perform better than the royal guard captain in the future?"", B3-Ch06a-Manipulation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Manipulation, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_b3_ch6_manipulators = 0)
choice(""Will this affinity help him when fighting against other aura manipulators?"", B3-Ch06a-Manipulation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Manipulation, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_b3_ch6_manipulators = 1)
choice(""What about the rest of our mages? Do you think they could learn aura manipulation too?"", B3-Ch06a-Manipulation, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Manipulation, v_checkpoint_rich = 0, v_b3_ch6_manipulators = 2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Manipulation
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_manipulators == 0) {
"Does this mean that Daren could perform better than the royal guard captain in the future?"

"Why on earth would you assume that?" Arraka's voice drips with derision. "Do you think the captain reached that tier of precision without innate talent of his own? Daren isn't catching the captain or the underground king—they have a thirty-year head start and a lifetime of discipline. Daren won't spend half that time drilling. He is far better off focusing on practical utility over raw domination. For instance, he could anchor the hilt to his palm with his aura during a clash, so an enemy can never strip his weapon."

"I can anchor it with my aura?" Daren asks, turning his broad palm over. "How?"

"Let me find a metaphor basic enough for a human skull," Arraka says. "How does the goblin frame these things for simpletons? Ah. You grasp the five currents from your fingertips now, yes? Instead of picturing them as rushing water, think of the quick-setting slime that giant snails secrete. You remember when Hadrik took a snail form and stuck himself to the arena dirt against that mammoth? Picture the aura pouring from your fingertips as that exact adhesive, coating the hilt while you grip it."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_manipulators == 1) {
"Will this affinity help him when fighting against other aura manipulators?"

"If you're asking whether he can overpower veterans with decades of field refinement just because he possesses raw aptitude, the answer is no," Arraka says. "If he directs the skill toward utility rather than brute force, however, it has real value. For instance, he could anchor the hilt to his palm with his aura during a clash, so an enemy can never strip his weapon."

"I can anchor it with my aura?" Daren asks, turning his broad palm over. "How?"

"Let me find a metaphor basic enough for a human skull," Arraka says. "How does the goblin frame these things for simpletons? Ah. You grasp the five currents from your fingertips now, yes? Instead of picturing them as rushing water, think of the quick-setting slime that giant snails secrete. You remember when Hadrik took a snail form and stuck himself to the arena dirt against that mammoth? Picture the aura pouring from your fingertips as that exact adhesive, coating the hilt while you grip it."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_manipulators == 2) {
"What about the rest of our mages? Do you think they could learn aura manipulation too?"

"Illuna possesses zero aptitude for it," Arraka says with a sharp cackle. "I tried drilling her back when she was my apprentice, and she couldn't grasp a single concept. As for the rest, they heard the goblin's explanation. They can test the current themselves and see if anything answers. Practical trial is the only measure. In any case, rather than fancy summoning tricks, Daren should learn an anchor that keeps his blade from leaving his grip in the first place. For instance, he could anchor the hilt to his palm with his aura during a clash, so an enemy can never strip his weapon."

"I can anchor it with my aura?" Daren asks, turning his broad palm over. "How?"

"Let me find a metaphor basic enough for a human skull," Arraka says. "How does the goblin frame these things for simpletons? Ah. You grasp the five currents from your fingertips now, yes? Instead of picturing them as rushing water, think of the quick-setting slime that giant snails secrete. You remember when Hadrik took a snail form and stuck himself to the arena dirt against that mammoth? Picture the aura pouring from your fingertips as that exact adhesive, coating the hilt while you grip it."

"Right," Daren mutters, skeptical. "Quick-setting slime. Here goes."

He closes his eyes for two breaths, letting his breathing settle, then opens his hand.

The broadsword hangs suspended against his open palm, defying gravity. Daren shakes his forearm vigorously. The leather-wrapped grip remains bonded flush to his skin, immovable against the momentum.

"That's remarkable," Daren says, flexing his fingers around the anchored hilt. "One detail, though. How do I release it?"

"Work that out on your own," Arraka says.

"Is this a deliberate exercise to sharpen my control?" Daren asks.

"No," Arraka says gleefully. "It's simply funnier this way."

Daren sighs, his scarred brow furrowing. "Of course it is."

He flaps his wrist back and forth, trying to fling the stuck weapon loose. Beside us, Kate watches his arm jerk with dry amusement.

"You have to guide the hardened aura back into a liquid state," Kate says calmly. "Like this."

She holds out her right hand, palm down. An ice dagger clings beneath her skin without falling. Slowly, the invisible bond softens like melting resin until the dagger drops and clinks against the flagstones.

"Not bad for an elementalist," Arraka notes. "What prompted you to try the same trick?"

"You suggested we test ourselves," Kate says evenly, crossing her arms. "Anchoring seemed more practical than summoning."

"Ah, I see it," Daren says. He turns his hand down. The sword's invisible grip loosens, and the heavy weapon drops to the stones with a ringing clatter. "Aura structure cannot simply vanish on command. You have to unwind the construct in reverse order. I had to liquefy the anchor before the bond broke."

"Congratulations, you deduced a primary law of aura craft," Arraka says as Daren retrieves his blade. "Take a biscuit."

"I would," Daren says, "if Barry hadn't turned so protective of his rations."

"I'm down to half a tin!" I say. "Those are for emergencies."

Flower tugs gently at Daren's elbow. "Should we keep moving? The teleporter is just down this passage, and the sick patients in the ward are waiting."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_manipulators != 2) {
"Right," Daren mutters, skeptical. "Quick-setting slime. Here goes."

He closes his eyes for two breaths, letting his breathing settle, then opens his hand.

The broadsword hangs suspended against his open palm, defying gravity. Daren shakes his forearm vigorously. The leather-wrapped grip remains bonded flush to his skin, immovable against the momentum.

"That's remarkable," Daren says, flexing his fingers around the anchored hilt. "One detail, though. How do I release it?"

"Work that out on your own," Arraka says.

"Is this a deliberate exercise to sharpen my control?" Daren asks.

"No," Arraka says gleefully. "It's simply funnier this way."

Daren sighs, his scarred brow furrowing. "Of course it is."

He flaps his wrist back and forth, trying to fling the stuck weapon loose. Beside us, Kate watches his arm jerk with dry amusement.

"Try guiding the hardened aura back into a liquid state instead of willing it to vanish," Kate says.

Daren pauses. "Let's see."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_manipulators == 0 || v_b3_ch6_manipulators == 1) {
He extends his right arm, palm down. The sword hangs below his hand for a heartbeat, then the invisible bond softens like melting resin, and the heavy blade drops to the flagstones with a ringing clatter.

"Ah, I see it," Daren says, scooping the sword back into his scabbard. "Aura structure cannot simply vanish on command. You have to unwind the construct in reverse order. I had to liquefy the anchor before the bond broke."

"Congratulations, you deduced a primary law of aura craft," Arraka says. "Take a biscuit."

"I would," Daren says, "if Barry hadn't turned so protective of his rations."

"I'm down to half a tin!" I say. "Those are for emergencies."

Flower tugs gently at Daren's elbow. "Should we keep moving? The teleporter is just down this passage, and the sick patients in the ward are waiting."

}
