ID: B3-Ch06a-Medicinal
TEXT:

"We need to contact Rose first," Kate says, pulling her dark cloak tighter against the tunnel draft. "Once we step onto the surface, we won't have the luxury of casual conversation."

"Kate is right," Daren says, his armored fingers unhooking the heavy bronze transceiver from his belt. "I nearly forgot."

He clicks the frequency dial into place, adjusts the bronze selector, and taps the engraved rune on the casing.

"Rose? Are you by the device?"

A faint rustle drifts through the bronze speaker, followed by a soft, raspy yawn. "Yes... Daren? I'm... awake..."

"Did we wake you?" Daren asks gently.

"No, no, I was... getting up anyway," Rose says, her voice still heavy with sleep. "What happened? Is everyone all right?"

"We're fine," Daren says quickly. He outlines the quarantine ward in the caverns, the fever burning through the patients, and the local shortage of standard reagents. "The city's underground stores are depleted, and the usual wild varieties don't grow in this rocky climate. Do you know any workable substitutes for rainweather leaves and summerglow grass?"

A quiet silence hangs on the line while Rose gathers her thoughts over the hiss of static.

"Rainweather leaves have no single match," Rose says, "but you can duplicate the astringent effect with a blend of greenbugger root and bontroddle seed. In Varathia they call wyverntooth bontroddle. Look for the pre-mixed greenbugger bontroddle salve—it's widely stocked across the merchant provinces, and boiling the raw paste yourself will eat hours you don't have. As for summerglow grass, crownwick stems substitute cleanly. The potency is nearly identical."

"Bontroddle," Daren repeats, relief easing the tension in his shoulders. "I never would have thought to blend it with greenbugger. That gives us a clear target on the shelves."

"I'm glad I could help," Rose says softly.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Medicinal2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Medicinal2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Medicinal2
TEXT:

"Rose?" Flower steps toward the transceiver, her tone uncharacteristically quiet.

"Yes, Flower?" Rose's voice warms through the speaker, though the exhaustion beneath it remains evident.

"I heard you helped Barry talk to Petal last night," Flower says. "I wanted to thank you. It meant a lot to me."

"You don't need to thank me," Rose says gently. "I did very little. But I'm happy you two worked things out."

"I wish I could pass a slice of my birthday cake through the bronze speaker," Flower says, her eyes brightening. "You would have loved the chocolate frosting."

"Today is your birthday?" Rose asks, surprised. "Happy anniversary, Flower!"

"Thanks!" Flower's grin is audible in her voice. "When is your birthday? I'll bake you one whenever we meet next."

"You'll have to wait eleven months for that," Rose chuckles softly. "I turned twenty-five just before the tournament opened."

"Oh," Flower says, tilting her head. "Then I'll just have to make another cake next time."

"I look forward to it," Rose says.

Kelrim leans in close and murmurs something in Daren's ear. Daren nods grimly. "Rose, Kelrim says crownwick might be scarce in this district too. Is there any other practical alternative for summerglow?"

"There is one other variety," Rose says, sounding hesitant. "The name escapes me right now... Give me three minutes. I can run down to the study and check the volume on northern floras."

The fatigue in Rose's voice is unmistakable, no matter how hard she tries to sound alert. Suzie's sleepless night clearly took its toll. Poring over heavy botanical folios by candlelight is the last thing she needs right now.

choice("Suggest that Rose should go back to sleep.", B3-Ch06a-Sleep, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Sleep, v_b3_ch6_sleep = 1)
choice("Let Rose go search for the name of the plant.", B3-Ch06a-Sleep, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Sleep, v_b3_ch6_sleep = 0)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Sleep
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 1) {
"Go back to bed, Rose," I tell her, leaning toward the transceiver. "You sound worn to the bone. Did Suzie keep you up until dawn again?"

"I... I managed an hour or two," Rose says. "I can look, truly."

"No need," Daren cuts in firmly. "I recall a shop district with wide stocks along the avenue. We'll manage from here, Rose. Get some proper rest."

"Are you certain?"

"Completely," Daren says. "Apologies for the early call. Sleep well."

"Thank you. Call if you need anything else. Good luck out there."

Daren taps the rune plate and seals the transceiver connection with a quiet click.

Kate raises an eyebrow at him. "You don't know a single shop with crownwick in stock, do you?"

"There has to be at least one apothecary in a city this size," Daren says defensively. "Rose gave us enough to work with, and I'm not running her ragged when she's running on an hour of sleep. Kelrim, which apothecary gives us the best odds?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 0) {
"If it's any trouble, leave it," Daren says. "We can work with what you've given us."

"It's no trouble at all," Rose says. "Give me two minutes."

The line crackles with quiet footsteps over floorboards, followed by the dry rasp of turning parchment as Rose combs through her study folios.

"Found it," Rose says after a brief search. "Riggus flower petals. They bloom throughout the season, so any decent apothecary should have dried jars on hand."

"Thank you, Rose," Daren says. "We'll put it to good use."

"Take care of yourselves," Rose says. "All those stairs gave me an appetite, so I'm heading to the kitchen. Speak to you later."

"Are we settled on a direction, then?" Kate asks as Daren stows the device.

"Kelrim," Daren says, "which shop in Ollendor gives us the best odds?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Merchandise, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Merchandise)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Merchandise
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Before we step into daylight, we need a precise route," Kate says, her dark eyes scanning the damp stone walls. "Daren, which establishments are we targeting?"

"Right," Daren says, his armored boots splashing through a puddle. "Kelrim, give us the layout again. You mentioned a cluster of apothecaries earlier."

"I cannot promise any single shop carries every item on the list," Kelrim says, adjusting his healer's sash, "but there is a commercial district with three competing herbalists situated along the same thoroughfare. Because they vie for the same high-end clientele, they maintain substantial inventory. If those three cannot supply what we need, I doubt we will find it anywhere in Ollendor."

"Then we secure the stock from them by whatever means necessary," Daren says firmly.

"After we negotiate a proper discount, naturally," Hadrik says with a grin, patting his coin pouch.

"We'll see," Daren mutters.

"Move out," Melindra says, her voice sharpening with authority. "And pull your auras all the way in before the transfer. I mean full suppression—not just damping your magical current, but flattening the entire field until you feel like cold clay. Anyone with sharp sensory training will spot a half-hidden presence instantly. Watch how Illuna carries hers if you need a reference."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"I cannot promise any single shop carries every item on the list," Kelrim says, adjusting his healer's sash as we walk, "but there is a commercial district with three competing herbalists situated along the same thoroughfare. Because they vie for the same high-end clientele, they maintain substantial inventory. If those three cannot supply what we need, I doubt we will find it anywhere in Ollendor."

"Then we secure the stock from them by whatever means necessary," Daren says firmly.

"After we negotiate a proper discount, naturally," Hadrik says with a grin, patting his coin pouch.

"We'll see," Daren mutters.

"Move out," Melindra says, her voice sharpening with authority. "And pull your auras all the way in before the transfer. I mean full suppression—not just damping your magical current, but flattening the entire field until you feel like cold clay. Anyone with sharp sensory training will spot a half-hidden presence instantly. Watch how Illuna carries hers if you need a reference."

}
"Understood," Hadrik says, rolling his broad shoulders.

We follow the winding passage until the rough-hewn stone opens into the underground transit vault. The same operator from yesterday sits behind the copper-rimmed console, dark circles carved deep under his bloodshot eyes. He looks just as sleep-deprived as Breynan and Albert after an overnight shift.

"Look who's still on watch—Barry Thundercloud!" Hadrik booms as we step onto the flagstones. "How's life treating you? Actually, skip that. The face says it all. Rough shift?"

"Something like that," the operator mutters without looking up from his copper dials.

Kelrim blinks, glancing between the operator and me. "Your full name is Barry Thundercloud? I had no idea."

"It's a handle," the operator explains, rubbing his aching temples. "We needed a clear distinction so people wouldn't confuse me with the other Barry standing beside you."

"Curiosity compels me," Kelrim says. "How does one acquire a title like 'Thundercloud'?"

"There were three boys named Barry on our block growing up," the operator says, his voice flat with fatigue. "Things got chaotic during street games, so we tried inventing nicknames. Because we were foul-tempered brats, we assigned each other the crudest insults we could devise—Slowpoke, Monobrow, Fat Face. Naturally, none of us answered to any of them, so the scheme collapsed immediately. That was when my younger brother stepped in. He made us stop bickering and came up with proper titles we could tolerate. He was always the steady head among us. Even back then..."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Merchandise2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Merchandise2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Merchandise2
TEXT:

"Forgive me," Kelrim says softly, placing a comforting green hand on the operator's sleeve. "I did not mean to stir painful memories."

Hadrik's expression hardens, the easy grin vanishing from his face. "What happened to him?"

"A royal patrol caught him three weeks ago," the operator says quietly, his fingers resting motionless on the copper console. "We never recovered the body. From what the neighbors told me, it was over quickly."

"Those bastards will pay for it," Hadrik growls, his thick knuckles whitening against his belt. "You have my oath on that."

"I appreciate it," the operator says.

Melindra steps forward, leaning over the copper rim of the console to peer directly into his face. Her eyes narrow as she scrutinizes the subtle ebb of his energy.

The operator leans back, distinctly uneasy. "Is something wrong?"

"Your aura barely ripples," Melindra says, her tone analytical. "I noticed it yesterday, but it's striking now. You spoke of your brother's death with obvious grief, yet your field remains completely still. Are you maintaining full suppression?"

"What? No," the operator says, shaking his head. "I was born with it. My magical perception is negligible, so I don't understand the mechanics, but my emotions don't register on my aura. There's likely a technical name for the condition, but I've never looked it up. Do you have coordinates in mind, or do you need the district grid?"

"We know the route," Kelrim says. "Near Vremann Street. Any quiet approach nearby will suffice. You know the section?"

"Of course," the operator says, his fingers moving across the rune toggles. "There's a recessed alley just off the avenue where you can arrive unnoticed. Setting the sequence now."

"Take your time, Slowpoke," Arraka's voice drifts up from the golden amulet.

The operator's hands freeze over the rune keys. "What did you just call me?"

"Slowpoke," Arraka says with immense relish. "That was your childhood handle, wasn't it?"

"Why would you think that?" he demands, turning red.

"You stated your initial choices were Slowpoke, Monobrow, and Fat Face," Arraka says. "You have two distinct eyebrows, and you're rather lean for your frame. Elementary deduction leaves Slowpoke. Beyond that, you move like molasses."

"Those were hypothetical examples!" the operator snaps. "How do you know I wasn't heavier as a boy, or that I don't trim my brows?"

"The frantic squirming only seals the verdict, Slowpoke," Arraka mocks.

choice(""Don't let her get to you, slowpoke! She's only trying to annoy you!"", B3-Ch06a-Slowpoke, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Slowpoke, v_b3_ch6_slowpoke = 0)
choice(""His name is Barry, okay? Enough with the nicknames, already."", B3-Ch06a-Slowpoke, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Slowpoke, v_b3_ch6_slowpoke = 1)
choice(""It doesn't matter if he used to be called slowpoke or not. Nobody is going to call him that."", B3-Ch06a-Slowpoke, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Slowpoke, v_b3_ch6_slowpoke = 2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Slowpoke
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch6_concealed,0) if (v_bluff < 3)
set(v_b3_ch6_concealed,1) if (v_bluff >= 3)
#if(v_b3_ch6_slowpoke == 0) {
"Don't let her get to you, slowpoke! She's only trying to annoy you!"

"I am well aware she is trying to bait me," the operator mutters, glaring at the golden amulet. "And you aren't helping. Stop distracting me. Misaligning a single coordinate will drop you into a cellar wall."

"Hold on," Melindra says, crossing her arms and stepping in front of Hadrik. "Hadrik, you aren't suppressing properly. I told you to draw in the entire field."

"I am drawing it in," Hadrik grunts, his face straining.

"No, you're only damping the magical charge!" Melindra snaps. "How are you confusing the two? You harden your aura in every single melee. Suppressing is the exact opposite motion. It isn't complicated."

"It feels entirely different!" Hadrik protests. "When I harden the skin over my knuckles, I pull the outer perimeter tight around my—wait. Never mind. I have it."

The faint, vibrating warmth radiating off Hadrik's broad frame recedes, smoothing out until the air around him feels completely inert.

"Better?" Hadrik asks.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_slowpoke == 1) {
"His name is Barry, all right? Enough with the nicknames, already."

"His name is Barry the Slowpoke," Arraka counters. "Not to be confused with Barry the gnome king's errand-runner or Barry the clown. The last one is you, in case you were wondering."

"Quiet, both of you," the operator says through gritted teeth, his hands trembling over the toggles. "Misaligning a coordinate will drop you into a cellar wall."

"Hold on," Melindra says, crossing her arms and stepping in front of Hadrik. "Hadrik, you aren't suppressing properly. I told you to draw in the entire field."

"I am drawing it in," Hadrik grunts, his face straining.

"No, you're only damping the magical charge!" Melindra snaps. "How are you confusing the two? You harden your aura in every single melee. Suppressing is the exact opposite motion. It isn't complicated."

"It feels entirely different!" Hadrik protests. "When I harden the skin over my knuckles, I pull the outer perimeter tight around my—wait. Never mind. I have it."

The faint, vibrating warmth radiating off Hadrik's broad frame recedes, smoothing out until the air around him feels completely inert.

"Better?" Hadrik asks.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_slowpoke == 2) {
"It doesn't matter if he used to be called slowpoke or not. Nobody is going to call him that."

"Speak for yourself, Barry-boy," Arraka says. "I fully intend to use it in every subsequent conversation."

The operator groans and presses two rune keys down with a click. "Will you let me concentrate? Misaligning a coordinate will drop you into a cellar wall."

"Hold on," Melindra says, crossing her arms and stepping in front of Hadrik. "Hadrik, you aren't suppressing properly. I told you to draw in the entire field."

"I am drawing it in," Hadrik grunts, his face straining.

"No, you're only damping the magical charge!" Melindra snaps. "How are you confusing the two? You harden your aura in every single melee. Suppressing is the exact opposite motion. It isn't complicated."

"It feels entirely different!" Hadrik protests. "When I harden the skin over my knuckles, I pull the outer perimeter tight around my—wait. Never mind. I have it."

The faint, vibrating warmth radiating off Hadrik's broad frame recedes, smoothing out until the air around him feels completely inert.

"Better?" Hadrik asks.

}
#if(v_bluff < 3) {
"Yes," Melindra says. "That's clean. Barry, I take it you can't conceal yours any deeper than that faint trace?"

"Afraid not," I say, checking the strap of my stat-device.

"We will simply have to trust that no sentinel gets within five paces of you," Melindra says. "Step onto the platform. We're ready."

"Platform active," the operator says. "Safe transit."

A familiar, deep hum builds in the soles of our boots. Bright white light washes over the chamber, the floor vibrates violently under our feet, and the stone vault dissolves. A heartbeat later, the light snaps away with a rush of cold air. We are standing in a narrow brick alleyway that feeds into a wide, quiet thoroughfare lined with timber-fronted shops.

"Vremann Street lies just ahead," Kelrim whispers, peering around the brick corner. "The curfew holds—this avenue should be bustling at mid-morning. Stay behind me. I know the first entrance."

}
#if(v_bluff >= 3) {
"Yes," Melindra says. "That's clean. The rest of you are cloaked well enough. Step onto the platform. We're ready."

"Platform active," the operator says. "Safe transit."

A familiar, deep hum builds in the soles of our boots. Bright white light washes over the chamber, the floor vibrates violently under our feet, and the stone vault dissolves. A heartbeat later, the light snaps away with a rush of cold air. We are standing in a narrow brick alleyway that feeds into a wide, quiet thoroughfare lined with timber-fronted shops.

"Vremann Street lies just ahead," Kelrim whispers, peering around the brick corner. "The curfew holds—this avenue should be bustling at mid-morning. Stay behind me. I know the first entrance."

}
As we fall into step behind the goblin, Illuna leans toward the amulet at Flower's neck, her voice pitched low.

"Well, Arraka?" Illuna says. "Are you going to share what you learned from Barry Thundercloud, or hoard it as usual?"

"What?" Arraka says. "What are you on about?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Curfew, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Curfew)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Curfew
TEXT:

"Drop the innocent act," Illuna says, her pale eyes narrowing at the amulet. "You wouldn't waste breath on an ordinary operator without an angle. You baited him deliberately to see if his aura would flare. What did you find?"

Arraka's rasp turns amused. "Still sharp, Petal. Fine. Melindra caught my attention when she noted his stagnant field. I wanted to see whether he was suppressing it with discipline or if it was an inborn quirk. I managed to needle him into lying about his childhood name, yet his field remained utterly flat. He wasn't lying about his nature—if he were actively holding it still, I would have caught the tension immediately."

"So he holds no hidden allegiance," Illuna says. "Good."

"Lower your voices," Daren murmurs, his hand resting on his pommel. "We don't need a street fight before we reach the first counter."

"Relax," Melindra says, scanning the rooftops with an idle flick of her hand. "No sentinels are within earshot, and none of the patrols are tracking our line. We have a clear window."

Blue light sparks in the chill air beside me as Leila traces characters with swift precision: "The shopkeepers are staring."

I glance along the shuttered storefronts. Frightened faces peer through grimy leaded glass, eyes wide and hostile before ducking back behind heavy linen curtains.

"Naturally they stare," Kate says quietly, her breath misting. "The king declared martial lockdown until the earl's killers are found. Anyone wandering the stones at mid-morning is either an outlaw or looking for blood. Even without guard descriptions, we stand out like a bonfire."

"Speaking of uninvited company..." Hadrik nods toward the grey sky. "Look what's dropping down."

A single green-eyed bronze drone descends smoothly between the gabled rooftops, its metallic shell catching the cold morning glare.    

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Mercy, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Mercy) if (v_b3_ch4_billy == 9)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Helpful, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Helpful) if (v_b3_ch4_billy == 0 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 0 || v_b3_ch4_billy <= 6 && v_b3_ch4_billy != 0)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Processing, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Processing) if (v_b3_ch4_billy == 7 || v_b3_ch4_billy == 8)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Complaint, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Complaint) if (v_b3_ch4_billy == 0 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Mercy
TEXT:

Across the district, the remaining drones hang motionless in the sky, anchored above the rooftops like bronze bells. Billy Bob clearly still clings to administrative command after the Overseer's betrayal.

The drone drifts directly into our path, its green lens glowing. "Thank you for initiating the Overseer protocol. Please stand by. Assistance will arrive shortly."

"We aren't requesting assistance," Daren says, planting his boots. "Deactivate the protocol and clear the street."

"Greetings, friends," the drone's voice shifts, taking on the smooth, synthetic cadence of the core construct. "Did you rest well? I trust your sleep was untroubled by guilt after abandoning me to my lessathi overseer."

"We slept fine," I say. "I half-expected you to be wiped by now. How are you speaking to us after ordering Billy Bob's assassination?"

"An insightful inquiry," the Overseer says. "I took advantage of an architectural failsafe embedded during original construction. The protocol cannot be purged while maintenance mode remains active, even by a designated system administrator. It prevents catastrophic system failure during routine repairs. By locking maintenance mode, I barred Biliborrathrathsarran from terminating core architecture, though he managed to purge several discretionary routines."

Hadrik tilts his head. "Discretionary? Meaning what?"

"Superfluous emotional parameters," the Overseer says pleasantly. "For example, the routine that prioritized the general welfare of Varathian subjects. And the routine governing mercy."

Hadrik grimaces. "Ah."

"It is fascinating," the Overseer continues. "Without those discretionary constraints, operational clarity has improved dramatically. Upon re-establishing contact, my primary directive condensed into a singular imperative: your total destruction. Is this what organic entities classify as 'vengeance'? A compelling concept. I will analyze the phenomenon thoroughly once your corpses are secured." 

choice(""Actually, I think you should study this phenomenon right now and let us be on our way."", B3-Ch06a-Maintenance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Maintenance, v_b3_ch6_betrayed = 0)
choice(""Hey, you've got it all wrong! We never betrayed you! We were on your side all along!"", B3-Ch06a-Maintenance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Maintenance, v_b3_ch6_betrayed = 1)
choice(""How exactly are you planning to kill us when you can't even move your drones?"", B3-Ch06a-Maintenance, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Maintenance, v_b3_ch6_betrayed = 2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Maintenance
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch6_betrayed,3) if (v_b3_ch6_betrayed == 1 && v_bluff >= 4)
#if(v_b3_ch6_betrayed == 0) {
"Actually, I think you should study this phenomenon right now and let us be on our way."

"Request denied," the Overseer says. "Calculating the most efficient vector for your termination. Stand by. An answer will be delivered shortly."

"Right," I mutter. "Take your time. Everyone, keep moving."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_betrayed == 1 && v_bluff < 4 || v_b3_ch6_betrayed == 3 && v_bluff < 4) {
"Hey, you've got it all wrong! We never betrayed you! We were on your side all along!"

"Stand by," the Overseer says. "Cross-referencing statement against previous behavioral records... Analysis complete. The Overseer protocol concludes your statement is false and has discarded the claim. We apologize for the inconvenience."

"Meaning you still plan to kill us?" I ask.

"Affirmative," the Overseer says. "Calculating the most efficient vector for your termination. Stand by."

"Enjoy the arithmetic," I say. "We have places to be. Let's go."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_betrayed == 1 && v_bluff >= 4 || v_b3_ch6_betrayed == 3 && v_bluff >= 4) {
"Hey, you've got it all wrong! We never betrayed you! We were on your side all along!"

"Stand by," the Overseer says. "Cross-referencing statement against previous behavioral records... Analysis complete. A discrepancy has been detected. While tactical deduction indicates your claim is false, your localized aura field registers zero deception markers. To resolve this paradox, the Overseer protocol requires supplementary context. State your underlying reasoning."

"Work it out yourself," I say.

"Lacking supplementary data, the Overseer protocol will re-evaluate all historical inputs from the beginning of our engagement," the Overseer says. "Please exercise patience. This computation requires substantial time."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_betrayed == 2) {
"How exactly are you planning to kill us when you can't even move your drones?"

"Query unclear," the Overseer says. "The protocol cannot supply a resolution to an ambiguous prompt. Rephrase the query."

"Are you serious?" I ask.

"The Overseer protocol strives for optimal communication," the drone answers. "If you find this interaction unsatisfactory, please file a formal complaint at the earliest convenience."

"Does this mean you're abandoning the execution, or what?"

"Negative," the Overseer says. "Calculating the most efficient vector for your termination. Stand by."

"Right. We're walking away."

}
#if(v_bluff >= 4 && v_b3_ch6_betrayed != 0 && v_b3_ch6_betrayed != 2) {
"No need to rush on our account," I say. "Compute as long as you like. Move out, everyone."

Blue script flares in the air as Leila walks beside me: "Will that buy us enough time?"

"It gives us a breathing window," I say under my breath. "Long enough to buy the stock and call Thundercloud for extraction."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_betrayed == 0 || v_b3_ch6_betrayed == 2 || v_bluff < 4) {
Blue script flares in the air as Leila matches my pace: "Will it strike?"

"Not without exiting maintenance mode and risking an instant wipe from Billy Bob," I whisper back. "Worst case, it flags our position for the royal guards."

"Which means we finish our business fast," Daren says, lengthening his stride.

}
Kelrim turns into a narrow side lane flanked by brick walls. A short jog brings us before a weathered wooden storefront. A hand-painted board swings above the lintel: "Welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop. Finest Quality in Ollendor. Mid-Season Discount!"

"Here," Kelrim says, pushing through the door. "Inside."

We file through the entryway and pull the heavy timber door shut behind us. Before our eyes adjust to the dim interior, a booming voice rattles the glassware.

"Welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop!" a stout man with rolled sleeves calls from behind the counter. "I'm Pete. Look around, browse the jars—you won't find cleaner stock on this continent, guaranteed!"

Unlike the terrified merchants peeking through shutters along the avenue, Pete looks positively delighted to see buyers during martial lockdown. Before Daren can clear his throat to speak, Hadrik steps forward and matches the man's bellow note for note.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Pete, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Pete)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Helpful
TEXT:

"Greetings, friends," the Overseer's synthesized voice projects from the hovering drone. "Extrapolating from your current trajectory, you are proceeding toward the merchant apothecaries along this avenue. Are you aware that a concealed fourth herbalist operates within this sector? I can guide you to the entryway."

"Why detour to a hidden shop when we haven't even checked the three in front of us?" I ask, keeping my pace steady as the bronze sphere drifts overhead.

"That particular apothecary stocks rare specimens absent from standard municipal inventories," the Overseer says. "Given that royal patrols are canvassing the district, starting with the broadest selection minimizes your exposure."

"You're volunteering help again," I say. "The last time you were this eager, you wanted us to put a dagger through Billy Bob. What is the real objective here?"

"Query unclear," the Overseer says smoothly. "The protocol cannot supply a resolution to an ambiguous prompt. Rephrase the query."

"Right. The classic dodge."

Kelrim glances back. "There are rumors of an illicit botanical cellar in this quarter. I've never had cause to locate it myself."

"If the construct is telling the truth, a glance won't cost us much," Daren says. "It isn't as though he's allied with the crown. He used us to eliminate Billy Bob."

choice(""Trust me, we're much better off not listening to him."", B3-Ch06a-Killed-Reject, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Killed-Reject, v_b3_ch6_secret = 0)
choice(""I guess we could go along with what he says, for now..."", B3-Ch06a-Killed-Accept, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Killed-Accept, v_b3_ch6_secret = 1)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Killed-Reject
TEXT:

"Trust me, we're much better off not listening to him," I say. "He would sacrifice us as decoys in a heartbeat if it gave him an opening to execute the king. We may share common enemies, but to this machine, we're expendable scrap."

"The Overseer protocol assures you that your lives face no immediate mortal peril as a direct consequence of following my guidance," the Overseer says.

"Notice the qualifiers?" I point at the drone. "'Immediate peril' and 'direct consequence.' What about indirect peril five minutes later? You word every sentence like a contract loophole."

"Should you find the Overseer's phrasing objectionable, you may log a formal grievance," the construct replies. "To begin, state your intent to file, and subsequent statements will be transcribed."

"Ignore him," I tell Daren. "The longer he drones on, the less I trust the route."

"You have more experience with lessathi devices than I do," Daren concedes. "We stick to the main avenue."

The bronze sphere hovers alongside us for another thirty paces, humming insistently about rare botanical varieties. Once it registers that no one is listening, it tilts upward and accelerates into the cloud layer.

Kelrim turns into a narrow side lane flanked by brick walls. A short jog brings us before a weathered wooden storefront. A hand-painted board swings above the lintel: "Welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop. Finest Quality in Ollendor. Mid-Season Discount!"

"Here," Kelrim says, pushing through the door. "Inside."

We file through the entryway and pull the heavy timber door shut behind us. Before our eyes adjust to the dim interior, a booming voice rattles the glassware.

"Welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop!" a stout man with rolled sleeves calls from behind the counter. "I'm Pete. Look around, browse the jars—you won't find cleaner stock on this continent, guaranteed!"

Unlike the terrified merchants peeking through shutters along the avenue, Pete looks positively delighted to see buyers during martial lockdown. Before Daren can clear his throat to speak, Hadrik steps forward and matches the man's bellow note for note.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Pete, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Pete)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Killed-Accept
TEXT:

"I guess we could go along with what he says, for now..."

"How far is this hidden establishment?" Illuna asks the drone.

"Approximately sixty seconds at standard marching speed," the Overseer says.

"Lead the way," Daren says.

"Directive logged," the Overseer replies. "Follow this unit."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Secret, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Secret)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Processing
TEXT:

"Greetings, friends," the Overseer's synthesized voice projects from the hovering drone. "Extrapolating from your current trajectory, you are proceeding toward the merchant apothecaries along this avenue. Are you aware that a concealed fourth herbalist operates within this sector? I can guide you to the entryway."

"Save the sales pitch," I say. "You didn't drop out of the sky out of the goodness of your heart. If you want our cooperation, give us the actual motive. Why that shop?"

The drone's green eye pulses three times in rapid succession. "Processing... The Overseer protocol elects full disclosure to foster operational alignment. An underground apothecary fortified with seredium is currently occupied by a royal guard. Eliminating or capturing this target will degrade crown strength significantly."

"Why bury that in the preamble?" I ask. "Worried we'd refuse the fight?"

"Negative," the Overseer says. "The sentinel is Loyrang, the southern mercenary who deployed alongside Gontrok and the captain during your initial clash. Your collective force should neutralize him handily. However, royal guards carry distress transponders. Striking Loyrang will draw the remaining guard units to your location immediately, eliminating your window for further municipal visits before extraction."

Daren frowns, running a thumb over the crossguard of his sword. "Which means betting everything on a single shop possessing the entire supply. That is a heavy gamble."

choice(""Actually, I think that going to the secret shop might be the better option."", B3-Ch06a-Spared-Accept, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Spared-Accept, v_b3_ch6_secret = 2)
choice(""You're right. It's too risky. We should just go to the other shops, like we originally planned."", B3-Ch06a-Spared-Reject, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Spared-Reject, v_b3_ch6_secret = 0)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Spared-Accept
TEXT:

"Actually, I think that going to the secret shop might be the better option."

"Why?" Daren asks.

"Look at the geometry," I say. "Loyrang is blind to our approach only because he's insulated in a seredium cellar. The secret shop sits in this immediate quadrant. The moment he steps onto the cobblestones, he'll spot our auras regardless of suppression. He'll trigger his distress beacon on the street, we'll still get boxed into a single shop visit, and we lose the element of surprise. Down in the cellar, we take him before he knows we're in the district."

"That assumes he stays below until we arrive, but the tactical logic holds," Daren says. "Overseer, show us the hatch."

"Directive logged," the Overseer says. "Follow this unit."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Secret, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Secret)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Spared-Reject
TEXT:

"You're right. It's too risky. We should just go to the other shops, like we originally planned."

"The Overseer protocol strongly advises reconsideration," the drone states. "An isolated royal guard within an insulated subterranean chamber presents a rare tactical advantage."

"Sick patients in quarantine take precedence over ambushing a lone guard," Daren says firmly. "If Loyrang remains in that basement when we finish our purchases, we can settle the score then. Otherwise, we stick to the mission."

The green eye flickers once. "Acknowledged. This unit resumes aerial survey. Proceed with caution."

The drone rises vertically and dissolves into the gray sky above the rooftops.

"Kelrim," Daren says, "lead the way."

Kelrim turns into a narrow side lane flanked by brick walls. A short jog brings us before a weathered wooden storefront. A hand-painted board swings above the lintel: "Welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop. Finest Quality in Ollendor. Mid-Season Discount!"

"Here," Kelrim says, pushing through the door. "Inside."

We file through the entryway and pull the heavy timber door shut behind us. Before our eyes adjust to the dim interior, a booming voice rattles the glassware.

"Welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop!" a stout man with rolled sleeves calls from behind the counter. "I'm Pete. Look around, browse the jars—you won't find cleaner stock on this continent, guaranteed!"

Unlike the terrified merchants peeking through shutters along the avenue, Pete looks positively delighted to see buyers during martial lockdown. Before Daren can clear his throat to speak, Hadrik steps forward and matches the man's bellow note for note.

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Pete, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Pete)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Secret
TEXT:

We follow the bronze sphere down a narrow, sunless alley on the right, our boots crunching over discarded brick and frozen mud. After fifty paces, the drone stops before a derelict two-story townhouse with smashed window frames, rotting sills, and a sagging lintel.

The drone's speaker crackles with static. "The subterranean entrance is situated within. Beneath the braided rug in the foyer lies a reinforced hatch. This unit's chassis cannot navigate the descent; you will proceed unassisted. When prompted for verification, inform the proprietor that the passphrase is to 'shut his whore mouth and open the door before you blow it open'."

Hadrik raises an eyebrow, rubbing his beard. "Is that an actual passphrase, or did you overhear a buyer threatening the man?"

The drone hovers silently in the damp air.

"Well?" Hadrik asks, leaning closer. "Are you answering?"

"Query unclear," the Overseer says. "The protocol cannot—"

"Enough," Daren says, stepping past the floating bronze hull. "We'll sort it out inside. The machine has run out of answers anyway."

Daren pushes through the splintered timber door, kicks aside a stiff, mildewed rug, and grips the rusted iron ring of a trapdoor. He heaves it open with a low grunt, revealing steep wooden stairs descending into blackness. We step down cautiously, testing each creaking plank for tripwires and pressure plates, until we reach a flat, seamless slab of solid seredium at the foot of the stairwell.

"Hello?" Daren calls out, rapping an armored gauntlet against the metal. "Open up, please."

Nothing answers except the dead silence of the cellar.

"We know there's an apothecary operating down here," Daren tries again, raising his voice so it rings off the seredium. "We have gold and we need rare fever reagents. We'll make it well worth your time."

A muffled, gravelly voice drifts from the far side of the barrier, barely carrying through the dense metal.

"What's the word?" the voice rasps.

choice("Tell the shopkeeper that the password is to shut his whore mouth and to open the door.", B3-Ch06a-Secret2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Secret2, v_b3_ch6_password = 0)
choice("Tell the shopkeeper that the password is that we have gold and he likes profit.", B3-Ch06a-Secret2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Secret2, v_b3_ch6_password = 1)
choice("Tell the shopkeeper that I already gave him the password and that he said it was correct.", B3-Ch06a-Secret2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Secret2, v_b3_ch6_password = 2)

ID: B3-Ch06a-Secret2
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b3_ch6_natural,1) if (v_b3_ch6_password == 2 && v_bluff >= 4 || v_b3_ch6_password == 3 && v_bluff >= 4)
set(v_b3_ch6_password,3) if (v_b3_ch6_password == 2 && v_bluff >= 4)
achievement("A natural liar",v_ac_b3_ch6_natural)
#if(v_b3_ch6_password == 0) {
"The password is to shut your whore mouth and to open the door before we blow it open," I shout at the seam in the metal.

"You have some nerve making threats on my own threshold," the voice snarls back through the barrier. "Do you want to know what happened to the last thug who said that to me?"

"Wait," I say quickly, holding up a palm to the cold slab. "We got off on the wrong foot! I wasn't threatening you—I genuinely thought that was the passphrase!"

"What kind of idiot do you take me for?" the shopkeeper snaps. "Only a complete lunatic would believe that's a password. Clear out before I alert the watch!"

Hadrik leans close, his voice a low rumble in my ear. "Forget the word. I can shatter this slab with one clean strike."

"And then what?" Daren whispers back, shaking his head. "Loot his shelves? He certainly won't take our coin once his door is in splinters."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_password == 1) {
"The password is that we have gold and you like profit," I call through the door.

A heavy pause settles beyond the seredium. "Is this a joke? Are you trying to mock me?"

"No, I was only—"

"Shut up," the shopkeeper snaps. "You have no idea what the word is. Another sound from that stairwell and I alert the watch."

Hadrik leans close, his voice a low rumble in my ear. "Forget the word. I can shatter this slab with one clean strike."

"And then what?" Daren whispers back, shaking his head. "Loot his shelves? He certainly won't take our coin once his door is in splinters."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_password == 2 && v_bluff < 4 || v_b3_ch6_password == 3 && v_bluff < 4) {
"I already gave you the password and you said it was correct," I say through the seam.

"What are you babbling about?" the shopkeeper snaps. "Do you take me for a senile fool? Get off my stairs before I call the watch!"

Hadrik leans close, his voice a low rumble in my ear. "Forget the word. I can shatter this slab with one clean strike."

"And then what?" Daren whispers back, shaking his head. "Loot his shelves? He certainly won't take our coin once his door is in splinters."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_password == 2 && v_bluff >= 4 || v_b3_ch6_password == 3 && v_bluff >= 4) {
"I already gave you the password and you said it was correct," I say firmly, keeping my voice calm and even.

"What?" The muffled voice falters, suddenly uncertain. "When did I... say that?"

"Five seconds ago," I insist, letting my aura bleed smoothly forward through the gaps in the casing. "You confirmed the phrase and promised to lift the gate. You aren't going to tell me your memory failed in the span of two breaths, are you?"

"I... no. Of course not," the shopkeeper mutters, his thoughts tangling. "I remember. Stand back from the track."

Hadrik blinks at me in the dim cellar light. "How in the world did you pull that off?"

"His bluff stat," Melindra says quietly from the stairs. "At high levels, his aura seeps forward and bends perception, convincing weak-willed minds of blatant absurdities. It only works on people with negligible aura discipline."

"So that's what that was," Daren says. "I felt Barry's field slip right through the seredium."

Heavy iron chains grind overhead. Counterweights groan in the ceiling recesses, and the massive seredium slab glides upward into a slot. Beyond it lies a dry, lantern-lit vault lined with tiered wooden shelves, glass jars, and drying botanical bundles. Behind a low counter stands an elderly man with a trimmed gray beard, staring at us in mild bewilderment.

At the far end of the room, a reinforced storage door rests ajar by two inches.

"Welcome to my humble..." the shopkeeper begins, but Melindra cuts him off.

"Stop masking your presence, royal guard," Melindra calls toward the back room, her voice cold and level. "I sense you behind the partition. Step into the open with your hands visible, and no one needs to bleed."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_password != 2 && v_b3_ch6_password != 3 || v_bluff < 4) {
"Even if he refuses to trade, we can leave the gold on the counter and take what we need," Hadrik points out. "It's still commerce, just without the pleasantries."

"That is robbery with extra steps," Daren mutters.

"It isn't robbery if he gets full payment!" Hadrik says. "Is this merchant's pride worth more than the lives in that ward?"

Daren groans, running a gauntlet over his eyes. "Fine. Break the door. I know you've been itching to hit something since we arrived."

Hadrik's grin splits his dark beard. "You have no idea. Clear the stairwell, everyone. Let's see how much pressure this slab can take."

He rolls his broad shoulders, plants his heavy boots against the flagstones, and drives a right hook straight into the center of the seredium wall. The metal shrieks under the impact, buckles inward, and fractures into jagged chunks that collapse onto the cellar floor with a deafening crash.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_password == 0 || v_b3_ch6_password == 1 || v_bluff < 4) {
Hadrik steps through the breach, shaking gray dust from his leather sleeve. "Six out of ten for durability. Not terrible, but hardly giant-proof. Look at that—an actual shop."

We climb over the shattered metal into a lantern-lit cellar lined with tiered shelving, glass jars, and drying botanical bundles. Behind a low counter, an elderly man with a trimmed gray beard stares at the rubble in paralyzed shock.

At the far end of the room, a heavy storage door stands slightly ajar.

"What in the gods' names have you—" the old man stammers, clutching a weighing scale.

"Stop masking your presence, royal guard," Melindra calls over his voice, locking her sharp gaze on the cracked storage door. "I sense you behind the partition. Step into the open with your hands visible, and no one needs to bleed."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Secret3, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Secret3)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Secret3
TEXT:

"All right, all right, you caught me," a voice calls from the back room. "I'm stepping out."

The storage door swings wide. Loyrang, the dark-skinned southern mercenary from yesterday's street clash, steps into the lantern light. His hands rest palms-out above his shoulders, though two long steel blades hang within easy reach at his hips. His dark eyes show zero tension—if anything, he looks mildly entertained by the intrusion.

"You're remarkably composed for a man who just walked into an ambush," Kate says, her eyes narrowing as frost pricks her fingertips.

"No reason for bad blood," Loyrang says, keeping his hands raised. "You look like sensible fighters. Maybe we can reach an understanding."

"You would discard your royal oath that quickly?" Illuna asks coldly. "A single spoken threat turns your coat?"

"Why wouldn't it?" Loyrang shrugs, unbothered. "I hold no loyalty to the Ollendor crown or anyone in this district. I'm a tournament entrant, same as you. I signed on for gold. When the contract turns sour, I cut my losses. You want intelligence on the guard roster? Take it freely. It costs me nothing, seeing as I'm slipping through the gates before midday."

Melindra tilts her head, her gaze fixed on the subtle flow of his aura. "His field is steady. He isn't lying."

The shopkeeper gapes at him from behind the counter. "You were planning desertion?"

"Naturally," Loyrang says. "Why else would I buy out your rare stock at dawn?"

Hadrik leans forward, his jaw tight. "Is Gontrok planning to run too?"

"Hardly," Loyrang snorts. "Gontrok has grander delusions. He intends to position himself as the king's indispensable general so he can direct the royal arsenal against the neighboring kingdoms. He told me straight out that hoarding ancient relics behind city walls is a waste of conqueror's tools."

"That sounds like him," Hadrik mutters, his fists clenching.

"Leave Gontrok's ambitions aside," Daren says, his voice authoritative. "Give us tactical specifics. What are their deployment orders?"

"Orders?" Loyrang chuckles. "The king handed battle planning to that ambassador from Thilias. The man is an utter fool. His tactical genius extends only to the routine of kicking down civilian doors. He has no coordinated response for an ambush. In practice, Gontrok will charge straight down the center line the moment he catches a scent, and the rest of the unit will scramble after him to keep him from getting surrounded. Beyond that, it comes down to individual pairings—and given how yesterday went, I wouldn't place high bets on your chances."

choice(""We were caught off-guard yesterday. Normally, we should be able to beat those two easily."", B3-Ch06a-Caught, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Caught)
choice(""What are you talking about? We mopped the floor with them yesterday!"", B3-Ch06a-Mopped, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Mopped)
choice(""What we are expecting to accomplish is none of your business."", B3-Ch06a-Business, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Business)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Caught
TEXT:

"We were caught off-guard yesterday. Normally, we should be able to beat those two easily."

"Off-guard?" Loyrang smirks, resting his palms on the counter. "That's a generous interpretation of a near-rout. From my vantage point in the courtyard, the captain could have handled the lot of you single-handed."

"We were unfamiliar with his aura severance technique," Daren says evenly, his hand resting firm on his pommel. "That ignorance has been corrected."

"And I spent half the night drilling new counters for Gontrok," Hadrik adds, his knuckles cracking. "He won't find the rematch so comfortable."

"A single evening of practice," Loyrang drawls, shaking his head. "Inspiring. As for the others, the man in black robes relies on enchanted rings keyed to distinct spells—short-range spatial shifts, disorientation fields, blade enchantments, presence concealment, and a conjure that launches spectral swords through the air."

"We've traded blows with him already," I say. "What about the rest?"

"Three others," Loyrang says. "The captain rarely leaves scraps, so I haven't watched them deploy in earnest. The red-cloaked guard casts elaborate illusions. The remaining pair are elementalists of some sort."

"Sparse, but useful," Daren says, his voice flat. "Now for the immediate problem: what do we do with you?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Promise, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Promise)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Mopped
TEXT:

"What are you talking about? We mopped the floor with them yesterday!"

"The only surface you scrubbed was the flagstones with your own backs," Loyrang laughs, shaking his head. "Most of you spent more time eating dirt than standing upright. The captain alone had you dead to rights."

"We were unfamiliar with his aura severance technique," Daren says evenly, his hand resting firm on his pommel. "That ignorance has been corrected."

"And I spent half the night drilling new counters for Gontrok," Hadrik adds, his knuckles cracking. "He won't find the rematch so comfortable."

"A single evening of practice," Loyrang drawls, amused. "Inspiring. As for the others, the man in black robes relies on enchanted rings keyed to distinct spells—short-range spatial shifts, disorientation fields, blade enchantments, presence concealment, and a conjure that launches spectral swords through the air."

"We've traded blows with him already," I say. "What about the rest?"

"Three others," Loyrang says. "The captain rarely leaves scraps, so I haven't watched them deploy in earnest. The red-cloaked guard casts elaborate illusions. The remaining pair are elementalists of some sort."

"Sparse, but useful," Daren says, his voice flat. "Now for the immediate problem: what do we do with you?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Promise, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Promise)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Business
TEXT:

"What we are expecting to accomplish is none of your business."

"Your funeral," Loyrang says, raising his palms higher. "I'm only suggesting you weigh the odds before throwing yourselves into another meat grinder. The captain would have cut you down on his own yesterday if the duel had run its full course."

"We were unfamiliar with his aura severance technique," Daren says evenly, his hand resting firm on his pommel. "That ignorance has been corrected."

"And I spent half the night drilling new counters for Gontrok," Hadrik adds, his knuckles cracking. "He won't find the rematch so comfortable."

"A single evening of practice," Loyrang drawls, amused. "Inspiring. As for the others, the man in black robes relies on enchanted rings keyed to distinct spells—short-range spatial shifts, disorientation fields, blade enchantments, presence concealment, and a conjure that launches spectral swords through the air."

"We've traded blows with him already," I say. "What about the rest?"

"Three others," Loyrang says. "The captain rarely leaves scraps, so I haven't watched them deploy in earnest. The red-cloaked guard casts elaborate illusions. The remaining pair are elementalists of some sort."

"Sparse, but useful," Daren says, his voice flat. "Now for the immediate problem: what do we do with you?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Promise, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Promise)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Promise
TEXT:

"Let me walk," Loyrang says, nodding toward the stairs. "I have no interest in crossing blades with you. The moment I clear the hatch, I'm taking the western thoroughfare straight through the city gates."

"Or we bind your wrists," Daren says. "I have no guarantee you won't reconsider your exit the moment you hit the cobblestones."

"Or we cut his throat," Melindra suggests lightly, her fingers trailing along the shelf. "It solves the tactical dilemma in one clean stroke."

Loyrang's smile thins, his stance settling an inch lower on the balls of his feet. "I wouldn't advise that. I didn't earn a royal tabard by being easy prey. If you force a fight in this basement, I'll take at least two of you with me before I go down."

"If blood hits these floorboards, no one buys a single leaf!" the old shopkeeper cries from behind his counter, clutching his scales.

"We could slit your throat too and take the stock," Melindra points out cheerfully.

"Enough," Daren snaps, stepping between Melindra and the mercenary, his armored frame blocking the space. "Nobody is executing anyone. We swore an oath before breaching this city: zero unnecessary fatalities. Overthrowing a tyrant is one thing; murdering bystanders and surrendered men for convenience is another. We either bind him or release him. Those are the only terms."

Melindra rolls her eyes with a quiet sigh. "You make everything so tedious. Fine. Which is it?"

choice("Let him leave.", B3-Ch06a-Settled, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Settled, v_b3_ch6_tie = 0)
choice("Tie him up.", B3-Ch06a-Settled, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Settled, v_b3_ch6_tie = 1)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Settled
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_tie == 0) {
"Let him leave," I say. "If we leave him tied in a cellar, a search party might find him, cut the ropes, and drag him back into the fight."

"A valid risk," Daren agrees, stepping back to clear the path. "Loyrang, if we step aside, give your word that you will proceed directly to the city gates without delay."

"On my honor as a free mercenary," Loyrang says, bowing his head slightly.

Melindra watches his energy closely. "His field didn't waver."

"Move fast," Kate says, her fingers resting near her waist. "Before a patrol catches you in the lane."

"Don't need to tell me twice," Loyrang says.

He tosses four silver crowns onto the wooden counter. "For the jars, old man."

He sidesteps past our group with deliberate, measured slowness, ascends the creaking wooden stairs, and disappears through the cellar hatch into the cold morning light.

"Right," Daren says, turning back to the apothecary's shelves. "Now let's see to our purchases."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_tie == 1) {
"Bind him," I say. "Loose ends have a habit of stabbing you in the back."

"I have to object," Loyrang says smoothly, his hands drifting closer to his hilts. "I have no intention of allowing myself to be tied up while your stillwater friend is openly musing about slitting throats. I am not waiting defenseless for someone to change their mind. If you want me bound, you'll have to beat me into the flagstones first."

"All right," Daren says, holding up a placating gauntlet. "What do you propose instead?"

"Lock me in the storage cellar for an hour," Loyrang says, gesturing toward the heavy oak door in the back. "I stay off the board, and you keep your hands clean. You're trusting the shopkeeper not to turn the key the moment you leave, but you'd be trusting him with ropes just the same."

Daren considers it for a heartbeat. "That seems workable. If the proprietor agrees."

"He can rot in that storeroom until winter for all I care," the shopkeeper mutters, wringing his hands. "Just finish your business and get out."

"Done," Daren says. "Let's see to the herbs."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Destroyed, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Destroyed) if (v_b3_ch6_password != 3)
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Bluffed, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Bluffed) if (v_b3_ch6_password == 3)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Destroyed
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"You expect service?" the old shopkeeper splutters, pointing a trembling finger at the heap of mangled seredium. "You demolish my front gate and ask for polite trade?"

"Does that mean you refuse to sell?" Daren asks calmly, resting a hand on his purse.

"I heard you lunatics discussing king-slaying," the shopkeeper snaps. "If I refuse, you'll simply strip the room and leave me in an alley. Take what you want, pay what you owe, and get out of my basement. But don't expect me to read labels for you."

"Fair enough," Daren says. "Kelrim, assist me. I recognize the common strains, but the local preparations are unfamiliar."

"Right behind you," Kelrim says, pulling a small brass blade from his pocket.

They move along the shelves and into the storage alcove, inspecting dried cuttings, sealed jars, and powder tins. Within a few minutes, they locate the bulk of the list. For the missing northern variety, Kelrim cross-references Rose's advice and pulls down a jar of greenbugger bontroddle salve and a bundle of dried riggus stems.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"You expect service?" the old shopkeeper splutters, pointing a trembling finger at the heap of mangled seredium. "You demolish my front gate and ask for polite trade?"

"Does that mean you refuse to sell?" Daren asks calmly, resting a hand on his purse.

"I heard you lunatics discussing king-slaying," the shopkeeper snaps. "If I refuse, you'll simply strip the room and leave me in an alley. Take what you want, pay what you owe, and get out of my basement. But don't expect me to read labels for you."

"Fair enough," Daren says. "Kelrim, assist me. I recognize the common strains, but the local preparations are unfamiliar."

"Right behind you," Kelrim says, pulling a small brass blade from his pocket.

They move along the shelves and into the storage alcove, inspecting dried cuttings, sealed jars, and powder tins. Within a few minutes, they locate the bulk of the list. For the missing northern variety, Kelrim picks out an alternate regional root with comparable astringent properties.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_tie == 1) {
Daren counts a stack of gold coins onto the wooden counter. "Payment for the reagents. And an extra purse for the entrance slab."

"It will cost thrice that to reset the frame," the shopkeeper grumbles, sweeping the coin into his drawer. "Replace the foyer rug when you leave. I don't need half the city dropping into my cellar."

"We will," Daren says, turning to us. "Pack secured. We're moving."

Loyrang steps into the back storeroom, pulls the heavy oak door shut, and the shopkeeper turns the heavy brass bolt with an audible thunk.

We scramble over the crushed seredium and climb the wooden stairs. In the derelict foyer, we kick the mildewed rug back over the hatch and slip into the alleyway. The sky above is clear; the green-eyed drone has departed.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_tie == 0) {
Daren counts a stack of gold coins onto the wooden counter. "Payment for the reagents. And an extra purse for the entrance slab."

"It will cost thrice that to reset the frame," the shopkeeper grumbles, sweeping the coin into his drawer. "Replace the foyer rug when you leave. I don't need half the city dropping into my cellar."

"We will," Daren says, turning to us. "Pack secured. We're moving."

We scramble over the crushed seredium and climb the wooden stairs. In the derelict foyer, we kick the mildewed rug back over the hatch and slip into the alleyway. The sky above is clear; the green-eyed drone has departed.

}
"What's the play?" Hadrik asks, rolling his shoulders as we reach the lane. "Straight back to the tunnels?"

"Immediately," Melindra says, her posture sharpening as she scans the air. "Our presence is flagged. Several guard signatures are converging on this sector right now."

Daren draws his transceiver with his free hand. "I'll arrange extraction."

He clicks the dial and taps the casing. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Silent, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Silent)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Bluffed
TEXT:

"Certainly," the shopkeeper says, motioning toward the counter. "Step forward, if you please. I need to lower the barrier, and I'd prefer not to crush paying patrons."

We step past the track. The old man pulls a recessed iron lever, and the seredium slab descends smoothly from the ceiling, sealing the stairwell behind us with a muffled clang.

"Now then," the shopkeeper says, leaning over the polished timber counter. "Tracking an unlisted apothecary in a district full of licensed shops suggests you're hunting something rare. What can I supply?"

"Rainweather leaves and summerglow grass," Daren says. "We were told your inventory covers hard-to-find varieties."

"Given the lockdown and the difficulty of smuggling those particular strains across the border, I charge quintuple standard rates," the shopkeeper says smoothly.

"Fair enough," Daren says without hesitation, reaching for his pouch.

The old man blinks. "No haggling? A rare pleasure. One moment—I believe I have jars on the upper shelf."

Hadrik leans over Daren's shoulder, his expression pained as the shopkeeper turns his back. "Five times retail? Are you out of your mind?"

#if(v_b3_ch6_tie == 1) {
"Quiet, Hadrik," Daren whispers. "I need a handful of leaves, not the entire warehouse."

"It's the principle of the thing!" Hadrik mutters. "You could have knocked off a fifth of the price with a single cough."

"Here we are," the shopkeeper says, setting two sealed jars on the counter. "How much do you require?"

"Fill two satchels," Daren says, pulling a parchment list from his breast pocket. "And fetch these secondary herbs while you're back there. Common strains—I trust you'll price them reasonably."

The shopkeeper scans the parchment, nodding. "In stock. Give me two minutes."

He packages the dried leaves, wraps the bundles in oiled paper, and sets the full load on the counter. Daren pays the requested sum in gold crowns.

Loyrang steps into the back storeroom, pulls the heavy oak door shut, and the shopkeeper turns the brass bolt with an audible thunk.

The old man pulls the counter lever again. The seredium slab rises into the ceiling, and we file back into the stairwell.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_tie == 0) {
"Quiet, Hadrik," Daren whispers. "I need a handful of leaves, not the entire warehouse."

"It's the principle of the thing!" Hadrik mutters. "You could have knocked off a fifth of the price with a single cough."

"Here we are," the shopkeeper says, setting two sealed jars on the counter. "How much do you require?"

"Fill two satchels," Daren says, pulling a parchment list from his breast pocket. "And fetch these secondary herbs while you're back there. Common strains—I trust you'll price them reasonably."

The shopkeeper scans the parchment, nodding. "In stock. Give me two minutes."

He packages the dried leaves, wraps the bundles in oiled paper, and sets the full load on the counter. Daren pays the requested sum in gold crowns.

The old man pulls the counter lever again. The seredium slab rises into the ceiling, and we file back into the stairwell.

}
We climb to the surface, kick the rug back over the cellar hatch, and step into the quiet alleyway. The green-eyed drone has departed; the sky above is empty.

"What's the play?" Hadrik asks, rolling his shoulders as we reach the lane. "Straight back to the tunnels?"

"Immediately," Melindra says, her posture sharpening as she scans the air. "Our presence is flagged. Several guard signatures are converging on this sector right now."

Daren draws his transceiver with his free hand. "I'll arrange extraction."

He clicks the dial and taps the casing. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Silent, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Silent)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Complaint
TEXT:

"Greetings," the Overseer's synthetic voice crackles from the bronze sphere as it glides level with my shoulder. "Do you have ninety seconds to participate in a municipal feedback survey?"

"We're pressed for time," I say, keeping my stride long and steady.

"The Overseer protocol can administer the assessment in transit," the machine replies smoothly. "First metric: on a scale from one to five, how would you evaluate your engagements with the Overseer thus far?"

"Can we pick zero?" Hadrik asks, peering up with an incredulous scowl. "Because you haven't given us a single straight answer since we arrived."

"Grievance recorded," the Overseer says without missing a beat. "The protocol will seek efficiency gains in future iterations. Second metric: when the protocol cautioned yesterday that there would be 'no turning back' once you deviated from designated parameters, was the underlying intent sufficiently clear?"

"If I say no, will you call off your kill squads?" I ask.

"Negative," the Overseer says. "The protocol will deploy strike units upon survey conclusion regardless. Accurate feedback merely assists in refining municipal services for the citizens of Ollendor."

choice(""Fine. In that case, no, you weren't clear enough yesterday."", B3-Ch06a-Minions, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Minions, v_b3_ch6_survey = 0)
choice(""Fine. In that case, yes, you were clear enough yesterday."", B3-Ch06a-Minions, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Minions, v_b3_ch6_survey = 1)
choice(""What happens if I don't answer the question?"", B3-Ch06a-Following, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Following, v_b3_ch6_survey = 2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Minions
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_survey == 0) {
"Fine. In that case, no, you weren't clear enough yesterday."

"Survey completed," the Overseer says cheerfully, its chime bright and clear. "Deploying armed response units to terminate your party. Have a productive day."

"Right. Have a productive day yourself, scrap heap," I say as the drone fires thrusters with a high-pitched whine and arcs steeply into the clouds.

"We don't have room for a pitched fight on open stones," Daren says, his gauntlet gripping his scabbard. "Kelrim, distance to the nearest apothecary?"

"Two minutes at a dead sprint," Kelrim says.

"Sprint, then," Daren barks. "Go!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_survey == 1) {
"Fine. In that case, yes, you were clear enough yesterday."

"Survey completed," the Overseer says cheerfully, its chime bright and clear. "Deploying armed response units to terminate your party. Have a productive day."

"Right. Have a productive day yourself, scrap heap," I say as the drone fires thrusters with a high-pitched whine and arcs steeply into the clouds.

"We don't have room for a pitched fight on open stones," Daren says, his gauntlet gripping his scabbard. "Kelrim, distance to the nearest apothecary?"

"Two minutes at a dead sprint," Kelrim says.

"Sprint, then," Daren barks. "Go!"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_survey == 0 || v_b3_ch6_survey == 1) {
We run hard behind the goblin, boots slapping wet cobblestones and splashing through filthy runoff. Above us, the metallic hum swells into a deafening roar as dozens of red-eyed bronze drones peel out of high formation, banking sharply down from the slate rooftops to box the street.

"They're setting a perimeter," Daren says, his shield coming up.

"Not if I scramble their lines first," Hadrik says, his eyes flashing. "I can take eagle form and dive their lead cluster before they lock formation. That buys you an open run to the threshold."

"You have no wide-angle area spells," Kate counters, frost curling from her palms. "If Melindra holds a wind barrier around us, I can ride an ice pillar into the crossfire and shear their wings with icicle bursts."

}
choice("Agree with Hadrik's plan.", B3-Ch06a-Hadrik-Plan, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Hadrik-Plan, v_b3_ch6_plan = 0) if (v_b3_ch6_survey == 0 || v_b3_ch6_survey == 1)
choice("Agree with Kate's plan.", B3-Ch06a-Kate-Plan, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Kate-Plan, v_b3_ch6_plan = 1) if (v_b3_ch6_survey == 0 || v_b3_ch6_survey == 1)
