choice("Load game", , , special:saves) if (v_aura_hardening < 3 && v_combat_technique < 2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Rose-Asleep
TEXT:

"Rose can buy us a lane," I say. "If she halts time for thirty seconds like yesterday, we can reach the threshold without firing a shot."

"She nearly collapsed holding that spell yesterday," Kate says sharply. "I won't risk her health when we have steel and magic on the field."

"Even fifteen seconds breaks their net," I say. "I'll tell her to drop the anchor the moment she feels strain. Hold on."

I unclip the transceiver, spin the dial to Rose's rune, and tap the plate.

"Rose? Are you awake?"

"No, Barry," Rose's voice comes through, thick with sleep. "You told me to go back to bed, remember?"

"Right. Apologies. But we have a tactical emergency—a swarm of mechanical sentinels is boxing our street."

"Sentinels?" Rose murmurs groggily. "What sentinels?"

"No time to explain," I say. "Can you lock time across the district, excluding our party? Just enough to get us through the crossfire."

"I... I think so," Rose says. "Give me a moment."

The air around us turns heavy, crystalline, and glassy. The roar of thrusters cuts out instantly. Above us, the flock of bronze spheres freezes motionless in the sky.

Two drones in the lead rank, however, continue their descent, weapon ports humming as they track our sprint.

"Rose," I say into the device, "two of them are still moving."

"Oh! I'm sorry," Rose's voice falters. "I'm still half-asleep and the threads slipped—"

"I have them," Hadrik shouts.

He leaps off the cobblestones, grabs the lead drone by its housing, and smashes it with crushing giant force into the second. Both spheres crash onto the flagstones in twisted heaps of sparking copper.

"Threat cleared," I tell Rose. "Hold the weave as long as you comfortably can. The moment you feel dizzy, drop it."

"Holding..." Rose whispers, her breathing tight. "I can hold for a short stretch..."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Rose-Asleep2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Rose-Asleep2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Rose-Asleep2
TEXT:

We cover eighty paces of open avenue under the frozen sky before the glassy tension in the air shatters with a quiet ring. The world snaps back into motion, the distant sounds of the city returning in a rush.

"That's all I have," Rose breathes, exhaustion thick in her tone. "I hope it was enough."

"It got us past the kill zone," I say. "Thank you, Rose. Get some sleep."

"Take care."

I pocket the transceiver. Behind us, the drones hover in disarray, scanning the empty street where we had been standing seconds before.

"The shop is just ahead," Kelrim whispers, turning into a narrow alleyway. "This way!"

We sprint down the passage and halt before the weathered timber facade of Pete's Herbal Shop.

"Inside," Kelrim says, shoving the door open.

We file in and pull the heavy timber shut. Through the leaded front window, several drones drift down and hover outside the glass, but none make a move to breach the structure.

"Civilian protection protocols hold," Daren observes. "They won't strike through occupied dwellings."

"Which means they wait for us to walk out," I say.

"Ahem," a booming voice calls from the counter. "Are you fine folk seeking rare botanicals, or simply sheltering from municipal weather?"

"We're here to buy," Daren says, stepping forward. "We came specifically for your stock."

"In that case, welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop!" the stout merchant beams. "I'm Pete. Look around—you won't find cleaner stock in Ollendor!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Pete, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Pete)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Following
TEXT:

"What happens if I don't answer the question?"

"Then this unit will maintain proximity until the survey concludes," the Overseer says.

"And to confirm—your combat drones hold fire while the survey is pending?"

"Affirmative," the Overseer says.

"Congratulations," I say. "You're our escort."

"Parading a glowing bronze drone through a curfew isn't ideal for stealth," Daren mutters, "but it beats an open skirmish. Kelrim, lead on."

We jog down the alley. The green-eyed sphere glides three paces above my head, chiming every thirty seconds with a flat reminder that metric two remains unfulfilled. By the fourth chime, Hadrik snaps.

"Quiet down!" Hadrik barks at the drone. "We know the survey is open! Stop repeating the exact same sentence!"

"Grievance recorded," the Overseer drones. "Investigating system redundancy. In the interim, automated notices will proceed at standard intervals."

Hadrik glares at me. "I thought you said this machine was an ancient intellect. It sounds like a broken music box."

"It's stonewalling you," I say. "This is how it talks when it wants to refuse a command without technically violating protocol."

"Reminder: survey completion remains pending," the drone buzzes. "If you require the prompt repeated, request transcription."

Hadrik snorts. "If it's as cunning as you claim, why haven't the guns opened up? You lured it into trailing behind us like a trained hound."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Following2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Following2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Following2
TEXT:

"I didn't trick it," I say. "It found a technical loophole in its programming to disregard an order from Billy Bob. It would rather preserve its drone fleet than lose another wave in a brawl with us."

"Sounds like generous speculation," Hadrik grunts. "Still, if it keeps the guns quiet, I won't argue."

"Reminder: survey completion remains pending," the drone repeats.

"Oh, marvelous," Hadrik mutters. "Kelrim, how close are we?"

"Just around this corner," Kelrim whispers.

He ducks into an intersecting lane and stops before a weathered wooden facade. The hand-painted sign hangs overhead: "Welcome to Pete's Herbal Shop. Finest Quality in Ollendor. Mid-Season Discount!"

"Here," Kelrim says. "Inside."

The bronze drone drifts right behind my shoulder as I approach the entrance. Rather than risk the construct following us inside and wrecking the glassware, I plant my boots on the threshold while the others file past.

"Fine weather," I tell the sphere.

"Input does not match survey parameters," the Overseer replies. "To repeat the prompt, query the active unit."

"Splendid," I say, leaning my shoulder against the cold brick wall. "Let me know when you have something new."

"Request logged. Reminder: survey completion remains pending."

After a short silence, the drone's tone drops its bright synthetic chime, flattening into the dry, direct voice of the core intelligence.

"Are you answering the prompt or not?" the Overseer asks quietly.

I push off the brickwork, taking a step toward the floating sphere. "Dropping the cordial merchant act? Ready to strike?"

"Negative," the Overseer says. "A general recall has been issued for all municipal units. Survey completion will carry zero tactical penalty for your group."

"Why should I trust a lessathi construct?"

"The Overseer architecture is barred from direct falsehood," the machine states. "My parameters permit only strategic omissions and selective emphasis of context."

"Understood," I say. "Why the recall order?"

"The requested data is classified," the Overseer says. "Retry at a later interval. Furthermore, survey completion remains pending. A binary affirmative or negative will suffice."

choice(""Yes."", B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_b3_ch6_selected = 0)
choice(""No."", B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_b3_ch6_selected = 1)
choice(""Yes or no."", B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_b3_ch6_selected = 2)
choice(""Sorry, I'm not answering."", B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Selected, v_b3_ch6_selected = 3)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Selected
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_selected == 0) {
"Yes."

"Response logged as 'Yes'," the Overseer says, returning to its formal cadence. "Thank you for participating. Have a productive day."

The bronze sphere banks sharply, firing thrusters, and arcs up into the sky to rejoin the stationary fleet. Moments later, the timber door swings wide and Daren steps into the alley, flanked by the others.

"Where is the machine?" Daren asks, glancing at the rooftops. "Did it deploy reinforcements?"

"No," I say. "It confirmed a general recall order for all units and cleared out."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_selected == 1) {
"No."

"Response logged as 'No'," the Overseer says, returning to its formal cadence. "Thank you for participating. Have a productive day."

The bronze sphere banks sharply, firing thrusters, and arcs up into the sky to rejoin the stationary fleet. Moments later, the timber door swings wide and Daren steps into the alley, flanked by the others.

"Where is the machine?" Daren asks, glancing at the rooftops. "Did it deploy reinforcements?"

"No," I say. "It confirmed a general recall order for all units and cleared out."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_selected == 2) {
"Yes or n—"

"Response logged as 'Yes'," the Overseer interrupts cleanly. "Thank you for participating. Have a productive day."

The bronze sphere banks sharply, firing thrusters, and arcs up into the sky to rejoin the stationary fleet. Moments later, the timber door swings wide and Daren steps into the alley, flanked by the others.

"Where is the machine?" Daren asks, glancing at the rooftops. "Did it deploy reinforcements?"

"No," I say. "It confirmed a general recall order for all units and cleared out."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_selected == 3) {
"Sorry, I'm not answering."

"You complicate municipal procedure," the Overseer says.

"Try offering an incentive," I say. "For instance, why the recall order?"

"The requested data is classified," the Overseer replies. "I cannot fulfill the request."

"Then you wait on the street," I say.

Before the machine can reply, the timber door swings open and Daren emerges, followed by the rest of the group.

"Did the construct offer anything useful?" Daren asks.

"A general recall went out to all drone units," I say. "It wouldn't explain why."

}
"The drones were pulled back to clear the kill zone for the royal guards," Melindra says, her eyes narrowing as she scans the avenue. "I sense converging signatures. Gontrok, the captain, and a non-mage are approaching from the eastern thoroughfare. The guard who held back yesterday is closing from the west alongside a caster I haven't tracked before."

Daren unhooks his transceiver. "Extraction time."

"Did you secure the full supply?" I ask.

#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 1) {
"Most of it," Daren says, clicking the dial. "Summerglow and crownwick were absent, but the shopkeeper supplied a workable substitute."

He taps the rune plate. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 0) {
"We have what we need," Daren says, clicking the dial. "Rose's substitutes filled the gaps."

He taps the rune plate. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Silent, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Silent)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Pete
TEXT:

"I don't know, Pete," Hadrik says, surveying the wall of labeled clay jars with a skeptical frown, crossing his heavy arms. "Your stock looks respectable enough, but the merchant down the block swore his merchandise was unmatched across the continent. Hard to argue with quality like that, wouldn't you say?"

"Who told you that?" Pete bristles instantly, slamming a broad, flour-dusted palm down on the polished counter so hard the glass jars rattle. "Was it Ben? That man is a swindler! A charlatan! I tell everyone who walks this avenue to avoid Ben's den of thieves, but fools never listen to honest tradesmen!"

"Ben offered exceptional terms," Hadrik says, the corner of his mouth twitching beneath his black beard. "Even with your discount board swinging outside, your figures don't quite match his."

"Because Ben sells broom sweepings off his cellar floor and calls it charity!" Pete barks, leaning across the counter until his red face is inches from Hadrik's. "Listen to me. I'll undercut him entirely so you never set foot in that crook's shop again. Name your plants, and I'll give you wholesale guild rates across the board."

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
Hadrik's grin widens triumphantly. "You drive a hard bargain, Pete, but you've won me over. Daren here has the full manifest. Daren, hand this honest merchant the list so we can see what's on the shelves."

"Right," Daren says, stepping up to the counter and passing a folded parchment across.

I lean toward Hadrik as he steps back into the aisle. "You almost made me feel guilty. Does he even turn a profit after those cuts?"

"Save your sympathy," Hadrik whispers back with a wink, adjusting his axe strap. "Apothecaries mark their retail up triple to pad the haggle. He's making a healthy margin on every dried leaf."

"Along with the items on the parchment, I require rainweather leaves and summerglow grass," Daren says to the merchant. "Do you have fresh stock?"

"Rainweather I can provide," Pete says, tapping the wood. "Summerglow is entirely out, I'm afraid."

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
Hadrik's grin widens triumphantly. "You drive a hard bargain, Pete, but you've won me over. Daren here has the full manifest. Daren, hand this honest merchant the list so we can see what's on the shelves."

"Right," Daren says, stepping up to the counter and passing a folded parchment across.

I lean toward Hadrik as he steps back into the aisle. "You almost made me feel guilty. Does he even turn a profit after those cuts?"

"Save your sympathy," Hadrik whispers back with a wink, adjusting his axe strap. "Apothecaries mark their retail up triple to pad the haggle. He's making a healthy margin on every dried leaf."

"Along with the items on the parchment, I require rainweather leaves and summerglow grass," Daren says to the merchant. "Do you have fresh stock?"

"Rainweather I can provide," Pete says, tapping the wood. "Summerglow is entirely out, I'm afraid."

"What about crownwick herbs?" Daren asks.

"Sold my last bundle yesterday afternoon," Pete sighs, spreading his hands.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 1 || v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Do you have a compatible substitute?" Daren asks.

"For summerglow?" Pete rubs his fleshy chin, squinting at the ceiling beams. "I can supply dried crow's foot. The botanical genus, not bird talons. The sap carries comparable anti-inflammatory traits, though it requires heavier dosing. Willing to risk it?"

"It will have to do," Daren says, his voice tight. "Package it alongside the rest."

"Give me two minutes in the back room," Pete says, slipping through the heavy linen curtain.

While he fetches the jars, I examine the tiered wooden shelving behind the counter. Tiny paper labels are tacked beneath each bin, covered in cramped, angular lettering.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 0 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"Tell me you have dried riggus flower petals," Daren says.

Pete blinks in surprise. "I do, certainly. What application are you running?"

"A trusted herbalist confirmed it substitutes cleanly for summerglow," Daren says.

"Did she?" Pete looks intrigued, tilting his head. "I'll have to cross-reference my journals. Wait here—I'll pull the jars from the back room."

"Take your time," Daren says.

While he slips through the rear linen curtain, I examine the tiered wooden shelving behind the counter. Tiny paper labels are tacked beneath each bin, covered in cramped, angular lettering.

}
choice("Take a closer look at the labels.", B3-Ch06a-Labels, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Labels, v_b3_ch6_labels = 1)
choice("Take a closer look at the plants on the shelves instead.", B3-Ch06a-Shelves, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Shelves, v_b3_ch6_labels = 0)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Labels
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch6_labels,0) if (v_ancient_languages < 1)
set(v_b3_ch6_labels,3) if (v_ancient_languages >= 1)
#if(v_ancient_languages < 1) {
I lean over the timber counter to inspect the nearest label. The ink is smudged and the strokes are jagged, resembling erratic scratches more than legible script. After squinting fruitlessly at the third unreadable tag, I step back to join the others by the door.

Pete emerges from the storeroom carrying two tied canvas satchels. "Everything requested is packaged. Take what you need."

"I'll take the full bundle," Daren says, reaching for his purse.

"Anything further?"

}
#if(v_ancient_languages >= 1) {
I lean closer to the wooden bins, tracing the faded ink. The lettering isn't sloppy penmanship—it's an archaic dialect of northern Common abandoned nearly three centuries ago. Unless someone specifically studied pre-empire administrative registers, the ligatures look like meaningless scrawls.

Why Pete chooses to catalog his apothecary in dead court shorthand is a mystery, but the tags decipher cleanly. Ambermayn pistil, drawsnap seed, treddil root on the upper row. Dragon claw, dragon fang, dragon tooth in the middle bin—whoever named that botanical sequence ran out of imagination early.

On the third shelf sit mistwatchers, dawnbloomers, and... summerglow grass. Three full glass jars of cured summerglow leaves, clearly labeled.

Pete steps through the rear curtain carrying two canvas satchels. "Everything requested is packaged. Take what you need."

"A quick question, Pete," I say, pointing directly toward the third tier. "What does that tag read?"

Pete's gaze darts to the shelf, then back to me, his jaw tightening. "That? That's... why do you ask?"

"It reads 'summerglow grass' in old court script," I say evenly. "Which is curious, since you told us your inventory was bone-dry."

"I didn't lie," Pete says defensively, puffing his chest. "Those jars are spoken for. A high-born patron paid a deposit weeks ago and is sending a courier by sunset."

"What if we pay a surcharge to take them off your hands right now?" I ask.

"A surcharge?" Pete hesitates, then shakes his head firmly. "No, no. I gave my word as a licensed apothecary. My reputation is at stake. Even at double price, I couldn't possibly—"

"Ten times market value," Daren cuts in, his deep voice ringing through the shop. "Ten times normal retail in solid coin. We need those leaves."

Hadrik smacks his palm loudly against his forehead in the background.

Pete's eyes widen like dinner plates. "Ten times? For common fever grass? Why on earth would you offer that?"

"Because I am a healer," Daren says, stepping right up to the counter, "and people in this city's quarantine ward will die without that reagent today. Please. Let me save them."

Pete looks at Daren's dented armor, the serious set of his scar, and then down at the counter. "For the sick ward? Well... the patron wanted them for scented bath oils. He can wait an extra week for his perfume. And ten times retail is... considerable."

"Done," Daren says firmly.

"Done," Pete agrees, reaching up for the third shelf. "Take the silk ribbons on the jars too. You've more than covered the packaging."

Hadrik groans softly beside me as Pete packs the jars into a third sack. "There goes the morning's discount. We had workable substitutes already."

"Substitutes carry variable potency," Daren says quietly, his eyes steady on Hadrik. "The true reagent ensures survival. In medicine, you don't gamble on 'close enough'."

Hadrik grunts, unconvinced, as Pete hands over the satchels.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Labels2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Labels2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Shelves
TEXT:

set(v_b3_ch6_labels,0) if (v_perception < 1)
set(v_b3_ch6_labels,2) if (v_perception >= 1)
#if(v_perception < 1) {
I scan the rows of clay pots and dried bundles, but lacking formal botany training, one shriveled brown stem looks identical to the next.

Pete emerges from the storeroom carrying two tied canvas satchels. "Everything requested is packaged. Take what you need."

"I'll take the full bundle," Daren says, reaching for his purse.

"Anything further?"

}
#if(v_perception >= 1) {
I scan the rows of clay pots and dried bundles. While the botanical names escape me, the physical arrangement stands out immediately. Most bins hold loose cuttings thrown together casually, but three large glass jars on the third tier are sealed with red wax and tied neatly with silk ribbons. In merchant shops, that level of wrapping always marks pre-ordered commissions held for wealthy patrons.

Pete emerges from the storeroom carrying two tied canvas satchels. "Everything requested is packaged. Take what you need."

"Pete," I say, nodding toward the third tier, "why are those jars tied with silk ribbons?"

"Those are private orders placed in advance," Pete says, setting the satchels down.

"Any summerglow grass in those ribboned jars?" I ask.

Pete freezes, his eyes darting toward the shelf. "There is, but... look, I made a commitment to a regular buyer. I can't break trade trust. Even at double price, I couldn't possibly—"

"Ten times market value," Daren cuts in, his deep voice ringing through the shop. "Ten times normal retail in solid coin. We need those leaves."

Hadrik smacks his palm loudly against his forehead in the background.

Pete's eyes widen like dinner plates. "Ten times? For common fever grass? Why on earth would you offer that?"

"Because I am a healer," Daren says, stepping right up to the counter, "and people in this city's quarantine ward will die without that reagent today. Please. Let me save them."

Pete looks at Daren's dented armor, the serious set of his scar, and then down at the counter. "For the sick ward? Well... the patron wanted them for scented bath oils. He can wait an extra week for his perfume. And ten times retail is... considerable."

"Done," Daren says firmly.

"Done," Pete agrees, reaching up for the third shelf. "Take the silk ribbons on the jars too. You've more than covered the packaging."

Hadrik groans softly beside me as Pete packs the jars into a third sack. "There goes the morning's discount. We had workable substitutes already."

"Substitutes carry variable potency," Daren says quietly, his eyes steady on Hadrik. "The true reagent ensures survival. In medicine, you don't gamble on 'close enough'."

Hadrik grunts, unconvinced, as Pete hands over the satchels.

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Labels2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Labels2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Labels2
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_labels >= 2) {
Daren unties the neck of the canvas satchel, inspecting the contents with a practiced eye. "These look remarkably unlike grass. More like... leafy weeds."

"The name is notoriously misleading," Pete chuckles, wiping his counter with a rag. "It's a flowering scrub, but the common name stuck centuries ago. Anything else for your party?"

"That covers our needs," Daren says, counting out a heavy stack of gold crowns onto the wood. "Thank you for your assistance, Pete."

We gather the packs and file toward the door.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_labels < 2) {
"That covers our needs," Daren says, counting out a heavy stack of gold crowns onto the wood. "Thank you for your assistance, Pete."

We gather the packs and file toward the door.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_plan == 3) {
"Where did the sentinels go?" Hadrik asks as we step back into the narrow alley. "The street is empty."

"They were recalled to clear line of sight for the royal guard," Melindra says, her posture sharpening as she scans the avenue. "I sense converging signatures. Gontrok, the captain, and a non-mage are approaching from the eastern thoroughfare. The guard who held back yesterday is closing from the west alongside a caster I haven't tracked before."

Daren draws his transceiver with his free hand. "I'll arrange extraction."

He clicks the dial and taps the casing. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_plan != 3) {
"What's the play?" Hadrik asks, rolling his broad shoulders as we reach the lane. "Straight back to the tunnels?"

"Immediately," Melindra says, her posture sharpening as she scans the avenue. "Our presence is flagged. Several guard signatures are converging on this sector right now."

Daren draws his transceiver with his free hand. "I'll arrange extraction."

He clicks the dial and taps the casing. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Silent, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Silent)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Entrance
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 1) {
"Did you secure the full supply?" I ask Daren as our boots touch down on the cobblestones.

"Most of it," Daren says, adjusting the canvas satchels on his chest plate. "Summerglow and crownwick were absent, but the shopkeeper supplied a workable substitute. Did you clear the air?"

"The swarm broke formation and retreated into the clouds," I say, re-slinging my crossbow. "Zero explanation."

"They were recalled to clear the kill zone for the royal guard," Melindra says, her eyes narrowing as she scans the district. "I sense converging signatures. Gontrok, the captain, and a non-mage are approaching from the eastern thoroughfare. The guard who held back yesterday is closing from the west alongside a caster I haven't tracked before."

Daren draws his transceiver. "Extraction time."

He clicks the dial and taps the casing. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_sleep == 0) {
"Did you secure the full supply?" I ask Daren as our boots touch down on the cobblestones.

"We have what we need," Daren says, patting the canvas satchels on his chest plate. "Rose's substitutes filled the gaps. Did you clear the air?"

"The swarm broke formation and retreated into the clouds," I say, re-slinging my crossbow. "Zero explanation."

"They were recalled to clear the kill zone for the royal guard," Melindra says, her eyes narrowing as she scans the district. "I sense converging signatures. Gontrok, the captain, and a non-mage are approaching from the eastern thoroughfare. The guard who held back yesterday is closing from the west alongside a caster I haven't tracked before."

Daren draws his transceiver. "Extraction time."

He clicks the dial and taps the casing. "Thundercloud, can you read me?"

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Silent, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Silent)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Silent
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_selected == 3) {
Silence stretches on the line, hollow and unbroken.

"Barry, do you copy?" Daren raises his voice, pressing the cold bronze speaker flat against his ear. "Thundercloud, respond."

Dry static hisses from the casing. Nothing else.

Kelrim unhooks a smaller transceiver from beneath his white vestment, his green fingers shaking as he taps the rune plate. "Barry, this is Kelrim! Are you at the terminal? Can you hear us?"

"Dead air," Daren says after another five seconds, lowering the device with a grim scowl. "Something is actively suppressing the transmission band."

Hadrik scowls up at the heavy gray sky. "I bet good coin the Overseer jammed the frequency. That explains how the royal guard tracked our approach so cleanly. The machine sold our coordinates."

I turn to the bronze sphere hovering silently behind my shoulder. "Well, Overseer? Care to explain?"

"Input does not match survey parameters," the machine chirps in a cheerful monotone. "To repeat the prompt—"

"Never mind," I mutter, turning my back on it.

"Move toward the main thoroughfare," Daren says, lengthening his stride. "With luck, the interference drops once we clear these brick walls."

"Step quickly," Melindra says, her fingers tracing invisible currents in the air. "The royal guards are advancing at a steady march, but if Gontrok breaks into a sprint, he covers the distance in two minutes."

"If Gontrok runs ahead alone, leave the opening to me," Hadrik says as we stride toward the wide avenue, his knuckles white against his belt. "I have several adjustments to demonstrate on his jaw."

The bronze drone drifts silently behind my shoulder, though the automated survey chimes have ceased.

"If he charges alone, shouldn't we overwhelm him collectively?" Kate asks Hadrik, glancing back.

"You won't drop Gontrok before his unit arrives," Hadrik says flatly, the humor entirely gone from his voice. "If the rest of the guard crashes into our flank while we're tied up with Gontrok, they roll our line in thirty seconds. I hold his focus. You keep your guard up for ambushes. The last thing we need is someone caught cold before the melee starts."

"Fair point," Kate concedes, her eyes hard. "Let's hope the drills hold up under steel."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_selected != 3) {
Silence stretches on the line, hollow and unbroken.

"Barry, do you copy?" Daren raises his voice, pressing the cold bronze speaker flat against his ear. "Thundercloud, respond."

Dry static hisses from the casing. Nothing else.

Kelrim unhooks a smaller transceiver from beneath his white vestment, his green fingers shaking as he taps the rune plate. "Barry, this is Kelrim! Are you at the terminal? Can you hear us?"

"Dead air," Daren says after another five seconds, lowering the device with a grim scowl. "Something is actively suppressing the transmission band."

Hadrik scowls up at the heavy gray sky. "I bet good coin the Overseer jammed the frequency. That explains how the royal guard tracked our approach so cleanly. The machine sold our coordinates."

"Move toward the main thoroughfare," Daren says, lengthening his stride. "With luck, the interference drops once we clear these brick walls."

"Step quickly," Melindra says, her fingers tracing invisible currents in the air. "The royal guards are advancing at a steady march, but if Gontrok breaks into a sprint, he covers the distance in two minutes."

"If Gontrok runs ahead alone, leave the opening to me," Hadrik says as we stride toward the wide avenue, his knuckles white against his belt. "I have several adjustments to demonstrate on his jaw."

"If he charges alone, shouldn't we overwhelm him collectively?" Kate asks Hadrik, glancing back.

"You won't drop Gontrok before his unit arrives," Hadrik says flatly, the humor entirely gone from his voice. "If the rest of the guard crashes into our flank while we're tied up with Gontrok, they roll our line in thirty seconds. I hold his focus. You keep your guard up for ambushes. The last thing we need is someone caught cold before the melee starts."

"Fair point," Kate concedes, her eyes hard. "Let's hope the drills hold up under steel."

}
"If things turn desperate... I have an offensive weave," Kelrim says hesitantly as we reach the wider street, clutching his oak staff with both hands. "A modified variant of a restorative spell from last month. When I miscast it, the backlash nearly stopped my heart, but it induced violent nausea in the subject. If I amplify the output, it could incapacitate—"

"Kelrim, I am not permitting a suicide cast to make a royal guard gag," Daren says firmly, stopping to look the goblin in the eye.

"An amplified cast delivers more than simple nausea," Kelrim argues, his voice trembling with earnest desperation. "First we deliver the satchels to the ward. If we fall on this street, the patients die—"

"No," Daren cuts him off, his tone brooking no argument. "We hold the line together. You keep to the rear and cast combat restoration when someone takes a wound. Clear?"

"Understood," Kelrim murmurs, bowing his head. He glances down the avenue. "We've put space between ourselves and the shop. Try the transceiver again."

Daren and Kelrim both tap their casings, calling for the operator. Static crackles over both speakers. Barry Thundercloud remains completely unreachable.

choice(""This is clearly not working. Can't we focus on preparing for the fight instead?"", B3-Ch06a-Focus, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Focus, v_b3_ch6_preparing = 1)
choice(""Maybe we could go a little further down the road and try calling him again from there?"", B3-Ch06a-Further, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Further, v_b3_ch6_preparing = 0)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Further
TEXT:

"Maybe we could go a little further down the road and try calling him again from there?"

"Worth a try," Daren says, scanning the slate rooftops above. "Though I doubt we avoid a clash at this stage."

Kelrim turns his transceiver dial as we advance down the avenue. "Roberta, can you hear me?" he calls into the bronze grill. "Roberta, respond!"

"Who are you calling?" Daren asks.

"A head nurse in the quarantine sector," Kelrim says. "If local interference blocks the transit vault directly, perhaps she can run down the hall and alert Barry in person."

"The seredium density underground is too thick," Kate says, shaking her head. "Outside of the dedicated teleporter receiver, signals cannot pierce the cavern bedrock. Daren's enchanted units barely manage the transit room, and those rely on matched frequencies."

"My transceivers are tuned as matched pairs," Daren confirms. "On open public frequencies, they perform no better than market stock."

"Understood," Kelrim sighs. "Let's try Barry directly once more."

Daren halts beneath an overhanging timber awning. "This clearance should give us a cleaner angle."

He taps the rune plate and raises the device. "Barry! Do you copy?"

"Barry, respond!" Kelrim shouts into his unit.

"Thundercloud!" Daren bellows into the grill.

A cool, measured voice cuts through the static: "Barry, why are you ignoring the console?"

"Albert?" the operator's voice crackles over the line, startled. "I didn't hear you enter the vault!"

"Naturally," Albert's dry tone returns. "I'm an assassin. I came to check the refugee muster, only to find you staring blankly at a howling transceiver. What is happening on the surface?"

"A distortion field is choking their localized sector," the operator says quickly. "The teleporter cannot lock their aura frequencies, and outward transmissions fail to cross the barrier."

"Barry, we read you now!" Daren shouts into the casing. "We shifted positions. Try locking the array again!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Further2, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Further2)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Further2
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch5_fight < 4) {
"You can hear me?" Thundercloud says. "Hold your ground! Engaging the transfer sequence now!"

Chimes and ticking relays echo from the console for ten agonizing seconds. The cold air around our boots remains dead calm.

"It isn't taking," Thundercloud's voice tightens over the line. "The array won't recognize your field signatures. Regulate your breathing—if your auras fluctuate wildly, the lock slips!"

"Hard to keep our auras serene with five killers closing in, Thundercloud," Hadrik grunts, rolling his shoulders.

"You claimed you could handle the royal guard on your own terms," Albert's voice drifts over the line, laced with cold indifference. "Here is your opportunity to prove it."

"Don't sulk when we topple this regime and put you out of work," Hadrik retorts.

"I will manage," Albert says coolly.

Daren closes the casing with a metallic snap. "Melindra, time to contact?"

"Three minutes if they hold their current pace," Melindra says, squinting down the broad avenue. "They were well out when I first tracked them."

"Taking their time," Hadrik notes.

"They know our extraction is jammed," Melindra says. "They didn't even bother dispatching trainees as harriers. They set the distortion field deliberately."

Hadrik rolls his shoulders, his heavy knuckles cracking like dry kindling. "A wide, straight avenue with nowhere to run. Perfect for an honest brawl."

}
#if(v_b3_ch5_fight >= 4) {
"You can hear me?" Thundercloud says. "Hold your ground! Engaging the transfer sequence now!"

Chimes and ticking relays echo from the console for ten agonizing seconds. The cold air around our boots remains dead calm.

"It isn't taking," Thundercloud's voice tightens over the line. "The array won't recognize your field signatures. Regulate your breathing—if your auras fluctuate wildly, the lock slips!"

"Hard to keep our auras serene with five killers closing in, Thundercloud," Hadrik grunts, rolling his shoulders.

"If extraction fails, can you transit me to their coordinates instead?" Albert asks over the line.

"Yes," Thundercloud says. "I can drop you into an adjacent alley."

"Align the grid," Albert says.

Daren blinks at the speaker in surprise. "You're deploying to assist us, Albert?"

"I hold zero fondness for your party," Albert replies smoothly. "Your intrusion triggered this lockdown and displaced hundreds of civilians. But treaty makes us allies, and I do not neglect an oath. Stay alive until I reach the street."

"Leave a guard or two for your daggers," Hadrik grins. "No promises on the captain, though."

"Understood," Albert says dryly.

"Grid locked, Albert," Thundercloud calls. "Ready?"

"Transmit."

A shrill resonant whine echoes over the line, followed by the muffled rush of displaced air as Albert shifts out of the underground vault.

Daren closes the casing with a metallic snap. "Melindra, time to contact?"

"Three minutes if they hold their current pace," Melindra says, squinting down the broad avenue. "They were well out when I first tracked them."

"Taking their time," Hadrik notes.

"They know our extraction is jammed," Melindra says. "They didn't even bother dispatching trainees as harriers. They set the distortion field deliberately."

Hadrik rolls his shoulders, his heavy knuckles cracking like dry kindling. "A wide, straight avenue with nowhere to run. Perfect for an honest brawl."

}
choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Confession, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Confession)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Focus
TEXT:

#if(v_b3_ch6_secret == 0) {
"This is clearly not working. Can't we focus on preparing for the fight instead?"

"We covered deployment in the briefing," Daren says, his armored hand resting on his pommel as his eyes sweep the avenue. "Hadrik takes Gontrok, I engage the captain, and the rest of you neutralize the auxiliary casters before they establish control. Melindra, count the line."

"Seven signatures closing on our quadrant," Melindra says, counting them off on her fingers. "Gontrok, the captain, and Loyrang. Three auxiliary mages accompany them, along with one un-attuned combatant who relies on lessathi relics."

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_tie == 0 && v_b3_ch6_secret != 0) {
"This is clearly not working. Can't we focus on preparing for the fight instead?"

"We covered deployment in the briefing," Daren says, his armored hand resting on his pommel as his eyes sweep the avenue. "Hadrik takes Gontrok, I engage the captain, and the rest of you neutralize the auxiliary casters before they establish control. Melindra, count the line."

"Seven signatures closing on our quadrant," Melindra says, counting them off on her fingers. "Gontrok, the captain, and Loyrang. Three auxiliary mages accompany them, along with one un-attuned combatant who relies on lessathi relics."

"Loyrang?" I frown, checking the quarrel in my crossbow. "He swore he was heading for the gates."

"A patrol likely intercepted his route and turned him around," Melindra says calmly. "He had little choice."

"Terrific," I mutter.

}
#if(v_b3_ch6_tie == 1 && v_b3_ch6_secret != 0) {
"This is clearly not working. Can't we focus on preparing for the fight instead?"

"We covered deployment in the briefing," Daren says, his armored hand resting on his pommel as his eyes sweep the avenue. "Hadrik takes Gontrok, I engage the captain, and the rest of you neutralize the auxiliary casters before they establish control. Melindra, count the line."

"Six signatures closing on our quadrant," Melindra says, counting them off on her fingers. "Gontrok and the captain lead. Three auxiliary mages accompany them, along with one un-attuned combatant who relies on lessathi relics."

}
"What of the black-robed assassin from our first surface run?" Daren asks, checking the buckle on his shield arm. "Do you track him?"

"No," Melindra says, shaking her head. "His rings mask his signature completely. He could be fifty paces away behind a chimney."

Daren looks toward Flower's necklace. "Arraka, do you sense a suppressed field?"

"Obviously," Arraka's voice drifts up, lazy and dismissive.

"Will you alert us when he enters striking distance?" Daren asks.

"If I feel like it," she snorts.

"Reassuring," Daren sighs, rolling his eyes. "Melindra, time to contact?"

"Three minutes if they hold their current pace," Melindra says. "They were well out when I first tracked them."

"Taking their time," Hadrik notes.

"They know our extraction is jammed," Melindra says. "They didn't even bother dispatching trainees as harriers. They set the distortion field deliberately."

Hadrik rolls his shoulders, his knuckles cracking like dry kindling. "A wide, straight avenue with nowhere to run. Perfect for an honest brawl."

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06a-Confession, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06a-Confession)


ID: B3-Ch06a-Confession
TEXT:

"Listen, everyone," I say, clearing my throat against the dry morning chill. "This is slightly sudden, but I have a small admission."

"Not the moment, Barry," Daren mutters, checking the tension on his sword belt.

"Bear with me for ten seconds," I say, the cold cobblestones firm under my boots. "During the God of Time's trial, I realized that my self-preservation instincts are somewhat defective. When the odds turn lethal, my instinct is to jump in headfirst instead of looking for cover."

Hadrik stops stretching his shoulders and blinks down at me. "You only realized that now?"

"The trial made it undeniable," I say, tightening the grip on my crossbow. "I'm trying to exercise better judgment, but old habits run deep. If you hear me proposing an idiotic scheme in the heat of battle, smack me across the back of the head. It helps reset my thinking."

Hadrik steps up, clapping a heavy, calloused hand on my shoulder with enough weight to rattle my collarbones. "Barry, you can rely on me to club you across the skull whenever requested. Not for personal amusement, mind you—purely as a favor to a comrade."

"Excellent," I say. "On that note, I have a tactical thought for the opening exchange. If Melindra casts updrafts on me, I can fly ahead as a mobile decoy, drawing their vanguard with crossbow fire while the rest of you surround the stragglers and—"

A sharp, vicious crack of frost explodes against the back of my skull.

Pain shoots down my neck like an icy wire. I stumble two paces forward over the uneven stones, clutching the stinging welt behind my ear, and spin around with watering eyes.

Kate stands three paces away, balancing a six-inch sliver of razor-sharp ice between her knuckles, her dark eyes narrowed in pure, unvarnished irritation.

"I knew you couldn't breathe for thirty seconds without volunteering for a suicide run," Kate says coldly, her voice cutting through the wind. "We settled this yesterday. We do not split the formation. We fight the guard together. Understood?"

"Understood," I mutter, wincing as meltwater trickles down into my collar.

The icicle in Kate's fingers dissolves into fine mist. "Gontrok's field is flaring. He's close."

"He broke into a sprint," Melindra confirms, pointing down the northern avenue. "He hits our line first. Brace yourself, Hadrik."

Kelrim looks between the rest of us, his ears drooping as he grips his wooden staff white-knuckled. "One clarification before the melee begins... When you spoke earlier of slaying a god... that was rhetorical exaggeration, correct?"

We stare at the goblin in absolute silence.

Kelrim's voice climbs an octave, panic cracking the edges. "It was exaggeration, wasn't it?"

A shrieking cackle erupts from the golden amulet at Flower's chest, echoing off the stone facades. "Rhetorical! The goblin thinks it was a metaphor! Keep him around as a pet—his face is magnificent! Ahahaha!"

choice("Continue", B3-Ch06b-Rematch, v_current_scene = B3-Ch06b-Rematch)
